ientific set but not fixed\" false; \"if fmt has fixed set but not scientific\" false; so exponent optional? That would be a weird degenerate case. Practically, `chars_format::general` is default.\n\nWait, there's actually a subtlety I recall: previously `chars_format::general` was specified to accept exponent. Let me not overcomplicate.\n\nMore importantly: the student's row frames \"hex\" under \"hex-число\" but the exponent part differs for hex: for hex floats, the exponent is `p`/`P` (base 2), not `e`/`E`. The student's exponent row only mentions e/E. For hex format, `chars_format::hex` — exponent is `p`. This is a potential omission but maybe out of scope.\n\n**Row 5: hex**\n- Student says: strtod accepts 0x, 0X; from_chars \"также воспринимает, но если bitmask `chars_format::hex`, то 0x 0X перестают быть префиксом 16ричного числа и становятся ошибкой\".\n\nCheck cppreference: \"if fmt is std::chars_format::hex, the prefix \"0x\" or \"0X\" is not permitted (the string \"0x123\" parses as the value \"0\" with unparsed remainder \"x123\")\".\n\nSo the student's \"становятся ошибкой\" is WRONG. It's not an error — it parses \"0\" and leaves \"x123\" as remainder. That's an important correction! The student wrote \"становятся ошибкой\" (become an error). Actually the cppreference explicitly says the string \"0x123\" parses as value \"0\" with remainder \"x123\". So no invalid_argument. The `ec` would be success (or... let me think: if the whole `[first,last)` isn't consumed, `ec` is still success, since ec only indicates no-match or out-of-range; partial parse is success). So the student's claim is incorrect.\n\nWait, but also there's nuance: for `chars_format::hex` WITHOUT... hmm. Also there's the question: does `from_chars` with default `general` accept `0x1.8p1` hex floats? Per cppreference, the pattern is identical to strtod except the listed differences. The listed differences only mention hex prefix issue when fmt is hex. With default `general` (not hex), does it accept `0x1.8p1`? Hmm. The standard says: for the general/scientific/fixed formats, the expected pattern is like strtod but... Actually I recall there's a well-known implementation quirk: `from_chars` with default `general` does NOT accept hex floats, because... hmm. Let me think.\n\nActually the standard wording: \"Expects the pattern identical to the one used by std::strtod ... except that: ... if fmt is chars_format::hex, the prefix 0x or 0X is not permitted\". This implies with non-hex fmt, the pattern is strtod's, which includes hex floats (0x...p...). But there's a known issue that the standard is somewhat ambiguous here. cppreference notes only the hex-fmt case. Hmm. There's LWG issue about it. Let me be careful — I should mark this as needing verification/uncertain, or just present what cppreference says.\n\nActually there's a known nuance: the standard says the expected pattern is the same as strtod's *for the selected format*. There's a discussion that `from_chars` with `chars_format::general` accepting `0x` is implementation-defined-ish. Let me check the `chars_format` page for details. Actually let me not overreach; I can mention it as a point to verify.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The standard (23.4.3? no, [charconv.from.chars]) says:\n\n\"Characters are parsed... The pattern is the same as in 21.3.4.3 [strtod] except that: ... specifies: if fmt has scientific but not fixed, exponent required; if fmt has fixed but not scientific, exponent not permitted; if fmt is hex, prefix 0x not permitted; plus sign not recognized outside exponent; leading whitespace not ignored.\"\n\nSo indeed with general (scientific|fixed), the pattern is strtod's pattern except those. So `0x1.8p1` would... be accepted? Since \"the pattern identical to strtod\" includes hex. Hmm, but then the hex row only applies when fmt==hex.\n\nEmpirically: MSVC's from_chars — I believe `std::from_chars(\"0x1p1\", ...)` with general returns 0 with remainder \"x1p1\" or parses 0? Let me think. Actually many implementations follow strtod and... Hmm, I'm genuinely unsure. cppreference example output only. Let me mark LOW confidence and suggest the student test empirically (they have MSVC!). That fits the course philosophy (§ 4.10: reproduce minimally, check hypothesis). Good teaching move.\n\n**Things the student MISSED (important):**\n\n1. **Locale dependence.** strtod: decimal-point character \"as determined by the current C locale\"; also \"any other expression that may be accepted by the currently installed C locale\". from_chars: \"locale-independent\" (explicitly in Notes). This is a major difference and directly relevant to their journal experiments with setlocale + strtod (decimal comma). Category: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\n2. **Error model / return value.**\n   - strtod: returns the value; errors via `errno` (ERANGE) and `endptr`; returns 0 if no conversion (ambiguity between \"0\" and \"error\" resolved by endptr). Overflow returns ±HUGE_VAL.\n   - from_chars: returns `from_chars_result {ptr, ec}`; `errc::invalid_argument` (ptr == first), `errc::result_out_of_range`; **`value` is unmodified on any error**. Throws nothing (noexcept). [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n   - The \"value unmodified on error\" is a real behavioral difference: strtod sets ±HUGE_VAL on overflow, from_chars leaves value untouched. This is exactly the \"model of errors\" architectural axis.\n\n3. **API shape / memory:** strtod takes a null-terminated `const char*` + optional `char**` out-param, writes through pointer; from_chars takes a `[first, last)` range, no null-terminator required, writes via reference. Relevant: their `parse_point` now uses `ptr != ptr_end` precisely because `string_view` need not be null-terminated (journal 2026-08-27). [ФАКТ].\n\n4. **Allocation & exceptions:** from_chars is non-allocating, non-throwing, and intended for high-throughput; strtod also doesn't allocate but may be affected by locale internals. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\n5. **inf / nan.** strtod explicitly parses INF/INFINITY/NAN/NAN(char_sequence). from_chars: cppreference's list of exceptions does NOT mention inf/nan, and the \"pattern identical to strtod\" suggests... Hmm. But empirically, I'm quite sure `std::from_chars` does NOT accept \"inf\"/\"nan\" — because the pattern described in the standard is the numeric pattern (sign, digits, exponent), not the inf/nan production. There's a known LWG discussion. Actually let me think harder.\n\nThe standard's [charconv.from.chars] for floating point says the pattern is as in strtod, except the bullets. But there's a separate note: LWG 3081? Hmm. I recall a cppreference note or a stackoverflow: \"std::from_chars(\"inf\") returns invalid_argument\". Let me consider: libstdc++ and MSVC both reject \"inf\"/\"nan\"? I believe yes — GCC's from_chars uses its own fast_float, which does NOT parse inf/nan. MSVC uses strtod-like? Hmm.\n\nActually this is genuinely important for the student's tasks: their journal 2026-07-06 says they added a finiteness check because \"from_chars читает их [nan/inf] как обычные числа\". Wait! The student wrote: \"по совету deepseek в с++ №4 добавила проверку на конечность числа (не nan / inf) т.к. fromchars читает их как обычные числа\". Hmm — that suggests the student observed from_chars parsing \"nan\"/\"inf\"? Or maybe they only meant that scientific overflow like \"1e400\" yields inf. Let me re-read: \"по совету deepseek в с++ №4 добавила проверку на конечность числа (не nan / inf) т.к. fromchars читает их как обычные числа\". Hmm, ambiguous. And 2026-08-27: \"сделаю случай для inf/nan в точках. причём если inf/nan среди X Y Z, то это будет отдельный вывод stderr 'не-конечное число'.\" And then they added tests: \"не-конечная координата; не-конечное число после X Y Z\". So it seems their tests feed e.g. \"1e400\" (overflow → inf) rather than literal \"nan\"/\"inf\" strings? Or literal? If literal \"nan\" and from_chars rejects it as invalid_argument, then the check would be different.\n\nThis is wor