 before \"число\" — matches \"Радиус должен быть конечным числом.\" (Радиус=6, должен=6, быть=4, конечным=8, числом=6). The mojibake \"������ ������ ���� ������� ������\" yes there's \"�������\"=7-8. OK so t4_radius_inf = \"Радиус должен быть конечным числом. Получено: inf\". Good — consistent: from_chars parsed \"inf\", isfinite caught it → radius_not_finite message.\n\nGreat, so everything is consistent: MSVC `std::from_chars` DOES parse literal \"inf\" and \"nan\".\n\nThis is a strong, non-obvious fact worth highlighting, and it corrects the common belief. And the student's code comment on line 33 says \"fromchars читает nan и бесконечность как обычные числа\" — so the student (with deepseek) already discovered this empirically. So actually the student KNOWS from_chars parses nan/inf. Hmm. So maybe I don't need to \"reveal\" it. But in their table they didn't include it as a row. And it's a place where strtod and from_chars AGREE (both parse inf/nan), so it's not a difference — but it IS a noteworthy \"same as strtod\" point, and it's a cross-platform risk worth noting (GCC's fast_float-based from_chars historically rejects inf/nan!). \n\nWait — but the student's tests pass on Ubuntu too (per journal). Hmm, do they? Let me reconsider. Journal 2026-08-30: \"прогнала чистый клон под виндой и под линуксом, в т.ч. с -j8 - не падает\". Tests added 2026-08-27/30 including inf/nan. And 2026-09-16 vcpkg runs. And journal doesn't record Linux failures on inf/nan. So presumably GCC from_chars also parses inf/nan, OR the tests weren't actually run with those cases. \n\nHmm, actually I recall GCC's libstdc++ from_chars float previously did NOT support inf/nan and there were bug reports; it was changed. Let me check: GCC PR 105441? \"std::from_chars for floating point does not parse inf/nan\"? I'm not certain. Actually fast_float's implementation: `fast_float::parse_float` — I believe it does handle \"inf\"/\"nan\" if the `parse_options` ... hmm. There IS a known difference: fast_float rejects leading inf/nan? Let me not speculate. Instead: flag as a cross-platform item to verify explicitly (run the inf/nan cases under the Ubuntu VM), mark [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ], because standard-conforming behavior may differ by implementation and this is exactly the kind of thing the course cares about.\n\nActually, I could fetch the libstdc++/GCC status. But maybe better to just recommend the empirical check. Given the course philosophy (documentation + experiment), and that I shouldn't guess APIs, I'll recommend verifying on the VM.\n\nHmm, but I could give more value by checking. Let me quickly consider fetching cppreference's \"compiler support\" or a known reference. Not reliable. I'll recommend the experiment. Actually — a neat idea: their test suite already encodes the expected behavior; running `ctest` on the VM would immediately reveal if GCC differs. That's the check. Just tell them: these two tests (inf/nan) are the ones to watch on GCC.\n\nHmm, but they said tests pass on Ubuntu. So maybe fine. I'll mention it as a thing to confirm rather than assert a problem, and note the standard's wording.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider: is there any real difference in inf/nan between strtod and from_chars? Per cppreference exception bullets: NO. But the *value* semantics: strtod(\"nan(2)\") gives quiet NaN; from_chars — does it accept \"nan(2)\"? Per \"identical to strtod pattern\", maybe. Not important.\n\nOK, now let me also verify the student's claim about default `chars_format`. They said \"(по дефолту scientific есть)\". `chars_format::general = scientific | fixed`. So yes. Let me confirm quickly — I'm confident. I'll mark [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] but note \"по cppreference/chars_format\".\n\nNow let me also double check one thing the student wrote: \"если это `scientific` и нету `fixed` - то е/Е обязательна\". cppreference: \"if fmt has scientific set but not fixed, the exponent part is required (otherwise it is optional)\". Correct. \"если это только fixed и нету scientific - то е/Е не будет воспринята\" → \"the optional exponent is not permitted\". Correct.\n\nOne more: the student's row says \"для восприятия е/Е в bitmask одним из параметров должна быть scientific\". Precisely: `scientific` alone → exponent required. `fixed`|`scientific` (=general) → optional. `fixed` alone → not permitted. So \"чтобы вообще воспринимать e/E, нужен бит scientific\" — hmm, with `fixed` alone, e/E is not permitted. So yes, you need the scientific bit for e/E to be allowed. Correct. OK.\n\nNow, additional differences the student missed (I'll present as \"не заметила\"):\n\nA. **Locale.** strtod depends on current C locale for decimal point and may accept locale-specific forms; from_chars is explicitly locale-independent (always '.'). [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]. Direct tie to their setlocale experiment (2026-07-24) and the § 4.3.B row.\n\nB. **Error model.**\n   - strtod: return value is the number; errors via errno (ERANGE) + return HUGE_VAL (overflow) / 0 (no conversion); endptr distinguishes.\n   - from_chars: returns from_chars_result{ptr, ec}; ec = invalid_argument | result_out_of_range; **value unmodified on error**; throws nothing.\n   - Consequence: on overflow strtod gives ±HUGE_VAL; from_chars leaves value untouched (e.g. 0) + ec. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n   Actually careful: on out_of_range, does from_chars leave value unmodified? cppreference: \"value is unmodified.\" Yes. [ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nC. **API surface / memory.** strtod: null-terminated + endptr out-param; from_chars: [first,last) range, no terminator needed, no allocation, non-throwing. Relevant to their parse_point using ptr != ptr_end. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nD. **Overflow vs underflow reporting.** strtod: ERANGE for both overflow and underflow (implementation for underflow). from_chars: result_out_of_range for \"not representable\". Underflow (e.g. 1e-400) behavior may differ by implementation — verify. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, НИЗКИЙ].\n\nE. **Apples-to-oranges row 1.** strtod is float-only; the integer counterpart in C is strtol/strtoll/strtoul. If comparing the integral overload of from_chars, compare it with strtol, where differences are:\n   - from_chars: \"0x\"/\"0X\" prefix not recognized when base is 16 (strtol accepts optional 0x for base 16); base must be 2..36 (strtol allows base 0 = auto-detect);\n   - minus recognized only for signed types (for unsigned integer value type, leading '-' is not recognized at all — whereas strtoul accepts an optional sign);\n   - still no leading whitespace, no plus.\n   [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] (from the integer exception bullets).\n\nF. **Hex-float exponent letter** is p/P, not e/E — the student's exponent row only covers e/E. Minor. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nG. **Rounding**: both correctly rounded (from_chars: at most two nearest values, round_to_nearest). Not a difference, but confirms \"одинаково\" on precision. Note the caveat: to_chars/from_chars round-trip guarantee only within same implementation. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nH. **Wording precision**: from_chars \"не падает\" — он не бросает исключений и не аварийно завершается; при ведущих пробелах просто не находит совпадения → invalid_argument, ptr == first. In their project they manually skip whitespace, so it's moot there, but \"упадёт\" is imprecise for the journal. [МНЕНИЕ/ФАКТ].\n\nI. Also: **strtod's endptr vs from_chars' ptr on no-match**: strtod sets endptr = str (nptr); from_chars sets ptr = first. Symmetric.\n\nJ. **Thread-safety/reentrancy**: strtod's dependence on locale makes it non-reentrant w.r.t. setlocale; from_chars is safe. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nNow the hex correction: student wrote \"становятся ошибкой\". Correct: with fmt == hex, \"0x123\" parses as 0 with remainder \"x123\" — NOT an error (ec success, ptr != last). [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] — direct quote from cppreference.\n\nBut careful nuance: with `chars_format::hex`, is exponent required? For hex, `p` exponent... the bullets don't require it. Whatever.\n\nAlso: the student's row \"hex-число | strtod воспринимает 0x, 0X | также воспринимает, но если bitmask hex...\" — the phrasing \"также воспринимает\" for from_chars under default is per the letter; but I'd flag implementation check. Actually hmm — wait. Let me reconsider. There might be a subtlety: with default `general`, does from_chars accept the `0x` prefix? The exception bullet is written as \"if fmt is hex, the prefix is not permitted\", which strongly implies otherwise permitted. So per standard yes. But actual MSVC/GCC? Unknown to me. The student can test in 30 seconds. I'll flag [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] about implementations, recommend experiment.\n\nNow, importantly, I should ask the architectural-axis question per the anti-sycophancy block: is this a local edit (journal table) or architectural review? It's a documentation/journal correction — local. The \"architectural axes\" here: none really, but the inf/nan finding touches their error-model (validation boundary) and cross-platform test stability — that's the one place where it's not purely cosmetic.\n\nAlso per the block: \"Перед тем как предлагать правку — задай вопрос: это локальная правка или нужен архитектурный пересмотр?\" — I'll note this is a journal/table correction (local), plus one item (inf/nan cross-platform) worth a separate test check.\n\nI should also give a micro-practical: a small `parse_diff.cpp` exe (голая версия, § 4.8) that for a list of inputs prints: strtod → (value, endptr offset, errno), from_chars → (value, ec, ptr offset). Run on MSVC and GCC. This empirically settles hex/inf/nan/underflow/+ and gives material for the table. That's very much in the spirit.\n\nShould I offer to write it? Plan mode — no edits. I can present a plan: e.g., add `tests/` or `sandbox/parse_diff` — but note § 4.9: no second build system; it'd be a CMake target. Hmm, adding it as a permanent target might be overkill; it could be a throwaway in a scratch dir under `build/` (gitignored) or a temporary exe. Actually the course says experiment in a branch, and don't keep dead code. I'll propose it as a temporary target or a scratch file, and let them decide.\n\nNow, per anti-sycophancy: I must not just praise. I should point out the incorrect row clearly.\n\nAlso: I should consider whether the student's table claim \"что распознаёт: float-числа (strtod)\" is missing hex/inf/nan — yes, notably strtod recognizes decimal, hex, INF/INFINITY, NAN/NAN(seq). The student's \"float-числа\" undersells it and hides the inf/nan question (which is actually what they rely on). So correct that row.\n\nLet me also double check the student's plus-sign row for strtod: strtod accepts leading plus. Yes: \"(optional) plus or minus sign\". And in exponent: \"followed with optional minus or plus sign\". Yes. Correct.\n\nNow, one more possible missed difference: **`from_chars` on `long double`/`float` overloads** — the student could note type coverage but minor.\n\nAlso **`from_chars` accepts `char` and all integer types**; for `bool`? No. Not needed.\n\nAlso **`from_chars` does not set `errno`** — include in error model.\n\nAlso **`from_chars` cannot skip to find a number; it must match at `first`** (no leading garbage). strtod also must match at first non-whitespace (after skips). Same-ish.\n\nAlso **`strtod` subject sequence may be affected by locale (\"any other expression accepted by the currently installed C locale\")** — a strtod-only behavior. Covered by locale row.\n\nNow compose the answer. Structure:\n\n1. Verdict: table mostly right; one row incorrect (hex), one row imprecise, one row apples-to-oranges; several significant differences missing. This is a journal correction (local), no architecture.\n2. Correct the hex row (quote cppreference).\n3. Correct \"что распознаёт\" row.\n4. Add missing rows (table).\n5. Note the inf/nan point — and the connection to their code/tests (they already know empirically; but flag cross-platform).\n6. Micro-practical.\n7. Final confidence check (per the block) — self-initiate: \"оцени уверенность 1–10\".\n\nI need to keep it reasonably concise but this is a detailed technical question; the user asked for corrections and omissions. A table is appropriate. I'll keep prose tight.\n\nAlso, per instructions, I should be careful with confidence labels. Let me assign:\n- Hex correction: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] (direct cppreference quote).\n- Locale: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n- Error model value unmodified: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n- API shape: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n- inf/nan accepted by MSVC: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] (their tests prove it); on GCC: [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] recommend VM check.\n- hex prefix with general accepted: [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] — standard wording implies yes; verify on both compilers.\n- Underflow: [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, НИЗКИЙ].\n- Integer side vs strtol: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nLet me write it. Since I'm in plan mode and they'll likely want the journal edit later, I'll end with a proposed plan (what to change in the journal) and ask whether to apply it once plan mode is off. Actually the user only asked a question; the \"plan\" is the corrected table. I'll offer the micro-practical as next step.\n\nLet me be careful not to over-format. I'll use a compact table for missing rows.\n\nOne more: I should double-check the claim \"from_chars: only the minus sign is recognized ... and only for signed integer types\" — cppreference integer bullet: \"only the minus sign is recognized (not the plus sign), and only for signed integer types of value\". Yes. So for unsigned, no minus. strtoul though: \"strtoul ... optional plus or minus sign\" and negates. So indeed differ.\n\nAlso base 0: from_chars base param \"a value between 2 and 36 (inclusive)\"; strtol base 0 = auto-detect. So from_chars has no base-0 auto-detect. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nAlso, one more missed: from_chars integer for base 16 does not recognize 0x — but wait, cppreference says '\"0x\" or \"0X\" prefixes are not recognized if base is 16'. Hmm, but strtol base 16: the standard says the optional 0x prefix is allowed only when base is 16 or 0. So strtol(16) accepts \"0x1F\"; from_chars(16) does not. Yes.\n\nLet me also mention: `from_chars` overloads are selected by the *type* of `value`, which is why \"две перегрузки\" — actually it's a set of overloads for each integer type + each FP type. Minor precision.\n\nLet me now write the answer. I'll aim for completeness but tight.\n\nRegarding the plan-mode: I must not edit. I'll explicitly note I haven't touched files and ask if they want me to (a) draft the corrected journal table, (b) create the temporary experiment target. And note plan mode.\n\nSelf-review confidence: I'll say 8/10, with the unverified parts listed (hex-prefix-with-general behavior, GCC inf/nan, underflow).\n\nLength: this deserves a fuller answer; the 4-line rule yields to \"unless the user asks for detail\" — they explicitly asked for a detailed correction. But keep it disciplined.\n\nLet me write.",
