          "text": "Five questions. Let me address each carefully with facts.\n\n**1. Underflow in strtod — is 0.0 returned?**\n\nYou asked me to check whether exactly 0.0 is returned on underflow.\n\nC standard (C17 7.22.1.3 strtod): \"If the result underflows, the function returns a value whose magnitude is no greater than the smallest normalized positive number in the return type; whether errno acquires the value ERANGE is implementation-defined.\"\n\nSo it does NOT necessarily return 0.0. It returns \"a value whose magnitude is no greater than the smallest normalized positive number\" — which could be 0.0, or a subnormal, depending on the implementation and on how far below the range the true value is. Actually the wording: \"If the result underflows (7.12.1), the functions return a value whose magnitude is no greater than the smallest normalized positive number in the return type\". So the returned value is ≤ DBL_MIN in magnitude. For a value that's genuinely tiny (1e-400), that's 0.0. For a value just below DBL_MIN (e.g. 1e-308ish), it might return a subnormal (which is ≤ DBL_MIN). So returning 0.0 is typical for gross underflow but not guaranteed in general; and importantly, whether ERANGE is set is implementation-defined.\n\nNewer C23 wording may differ slightly. cppreference says: \"If the converted value falls out of range of corresponding return type, range error occurs (errno is set to ERANGE) and HUGE_VAL, HUGE_VALF or HUGE_VALL is returned.\" Hmm — cppreference's Return value section lumps overflow and underflow, saying HUGE_VAL is returned. That's actually imprecise/wrong for underflow. Let me be careful.\n\nActually the cppreference Return value says: \"Floating-point value corresponding to the contents of str on success. If the converted value falls out of range of corresponding return type, range error occurs (errno is set to ERANGE) and HUGE_VAL, HUGE_VALF or HUGE_VALL is returned. If no conversion can be performed, 0 is returned.\"\n\nHmm so cppreference says HUGE_VAL for \"out of range\" generally. But the C standard distinguishes: overflow → HUGE_VAL; underflow → magnitude ≤ smallest normalized, ERANGE implementation-defined. cppreference is being loose here. This is actually a place where the primary standard (C17 §7.22.1.3) is more precise than cppreference's summary.\n\nSo: answer to Q1: **No, not exactly 0.0 guaranteed.** Per the C standard, on underflow the return is \"a value whose magnitude is no greater than the smallest normalized positive number\" — 0.0 is the common result for gross underflow, but a subnormal is also possible; and whether ERANGE is set is implementation-defined. cppreference's summary conflates overflow and underflow (says HUGE_VAL for any out-of-range) — that's the loose part. So the student should NOT write \"0.0 при underflow\"; they should write the standard's wording, and verify empirically for a specific input.\n\nConfidence: [ФАКТ про формулировку стандарта, ВЫСОКИЙ]. But I haven't re-fetched the C standard text. I fetched cppreference strtof page. Let me maybe verify the standard wording. I could fetch the C standard draft (N2310/N3096) or rely on knowledge. Hmm. Being careful: the exact phrase \"a value whose magnitude is no greater than the smallest normalized positive number in the return type\" is from C99 7.20.1.3. I'm fairly confident. I can cite it as [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] but note it's from the standard, not cppreference (cppreference oversimplifies).\n\nActually, let me double-check by fetching something authoritative. I could fetch the C standard draft text. Let me consider: perhaps worth one fetch to be safe, since the user explicitly asked \"проверь меня\". Let me fetch cppreference C++ strtof page too? Or the C standard draft. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about whether to fetch. The user says \"проверь меня, что именно 0.0 будет возвращён при underflow.\" So they're asking me to verify. I should verify from an authoritative source. cppreference (which I already have) does