d. But base C (without Annex F) may allow more rounding error? Hmm. Actually I think there's a footnote or recommended practice saying \"the result should be correctly rounded\" or \"the value is the nearest\". Let me fetch the \"Recommended practice\" part right after p8.\n\nThis matters for the claim \"both correctly rounded\". Let me extract it.\n\nActually for the student's table, the key point is: the row isn't a *difference* and the round-trip note is about to_chars/from_chars. Whether C mandates correct rounding for decimal strtod is a side nuance. But since the student asks \"are there differences?\", I should be accurate: from_chars explicitly mandates round_to_nearest; strtod mandates correct rounding for hex form, and for decimal it's \"recommended practice\" (and required under Annex F/IEEE). So there IS a small guarantee-strength difference, but not a value difference in practice.\n\nLet me fetch the recommended practice text to be precise. One more command.\n\nLet me extract around 'Recommended practice' after 7.22.1.3.\n\nSearch for the second 'Recommended practice' near index of strtod section. Let me find \"correctly rounded\" occurrences.\n\nLet me run.\n\nAlso I should consider Q5's \"в пределах одной реализации\" — cppreference note is about to_chars/from_chars. Let me verify the note is indeed about to_chars/from_chars and not strtod. From the fetch earlier (from_chars page Notes): \"The guarantee that std::from_chars can recover every floating-point value formatted by std::to_chars exactly is only provided if both functions are from the same implementation.\" Yes. Confirmed. So it's about to_chars/from_chars, nothing to do with strtod. Good.\n\nLet me get the recommended practice.\n\nLet me run one more command.",
