oad — implementation-defined; чтобы его прочитать, надо смотреть биты мантиссы (не переносимо). Example: strtod(\"NAN(2)\") — from the cppreference example output, \"nan(2)\" → nan, consumed.\nAlso: `nan(\"...\")` function (7.12.11.2) is equivalent to strtod(\"NAN(...)\").\nPractical: for your tasks it's just another spelling of a non-finite token; your `isfinite` catches it.\n\n**Q3: \"побочные эффекты\" — duplication?**\nYou're right — drop the full row. [МНЕНИЕ/согласна]\nReasons:\n- locale → already row \"разделитель\" + row 1 (strtod \"любые др. выражения...\") — and I just verified from the standard ¶6: \"In other than the \"C\" locale, additional locale-specific subject sequence forms may be accepted.\" So row 1 is well-founded.\n- errno → already in the \"выход за диапазон\" row (strtod: errno=ERANGE) and from_chars rows (ec).\n- The remaining bits (non-throwing/non-allocating) — and here's the key: **against strtod they are not differences at all** (C function, no C++ exceptions, no allocation). The property matters vs `std::stod`/`operator>>`, not vs strtod. So it doesn't belong in a \"strtod vs from_chars\" table.\nSo: no new row. At most a one-line footnote if you want.\n\n**Q4: integer overload later** — ok, accept. Nothing to add. (I'll just note that I'll re-raise at final review if it doesn't happen; but they said it'll be done. Fine.)\n\n**Q5: \"точность\" row — no difference; and the round-trip note meaning.**\nYou're right, and here's the precise picture:\n- Value: both should give the same result on a conforming impl; there's no *value* difference. So in a *differences* table the row is noise.\n- But guarantee strength DOES differ (this is a real, if subtle, asymmetry):\n  - `from_chars` (C++): hard requirement — \"one of at most two floating-point values closest ..., after rounding according to round_to_nearest\" (always).\n  - `strtod` (C): correctly rounded is a **hard** requirement only for the **hex** form (when FLT_RADIX is a power of 2); for the **decimal** form it's only **Recommended practice**, and only for ≤ `DECIMAL_DIG` significant digits (C11 7.22.1.3 ¶5, Recommended practice ¶9). Annex F tightens it to correctly rounded for ≤ DECIMAL_DIG digits.\n  - In practice MSVC/glibc are correctly rounded → no observable difference. [ФАКТ про стандарт, ВЫСОКИЙ; практика — МНЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n- \"гарантирован только в пределах одной реализации\" — that's a **different** property entirely: it's the `to_chars`↔`from_chars` round-trip guarantee (from_chars Notes): \"The guarantee that std::from_chars can recover every floating-point value formatted by std::to_chars exactly is only provided if both functions are from the same implementation.\" Meaning: serialize with `to_chars` MSVC → parse with `from_chars` GCC may not reproduce the exact double, because the \"shortest decimal\" choices can differ between implementations. It has **nothing to do with strtod**. So it must not be a \"strtod vs from_chars\" row at all.\n\nSo recommendation: remove the \"точность\" row from the differences table; if you want to keep the thought, put it under a separate \"общее / не отличие\" note with the two standard clauses above.\n\nNow, also I should maybe mention: the student's own source (cppreference) is loose about underflow — a good \"meta\" finding worth a journal line: \"пересказ в cppreference в одном месте неточен; стандарт C — точнее\".\n\nNow the final review per anti-sycophancy: give confidence 1-10 and unverified items.\n\nUnverified:\n- Whether MSVC/glibc return 0.0 vs subnormal for specific inputs (1e-400, 1e-320) and whether they set ERANGE — experiment.\n- from_chars underflow behavior (still open).\n- hex prefix under general (still open).\n- GCC inf/nan (still open).\n\nAlso: I should note that I'm in plan mode and made no changes.\n\nLet me also double check one thing about Q2: is `nondigit` really including underscore? C11 6.4.2.1: nondigit: one of _ a b c d ... So yes. But the extracted grammar showed \"d