          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me now address the three points.\n\n**Point 1: What is \"implementation-defined value\" — explain simply.**\n\nKey distinction in C standard:\n- **Unspecified behavior**: implementation may choose any behavior; not required to document. (e.g., order of evaluation of function arguments)\n- **Implementation-defined behavior**: implementation must choose a behavior AND document it (e.g., in the compiler/library docs). So it's *deterministic and documented per implementation*, but varies between implementations.\n- **Undefined behavior**: anything can happen; no requirements. (UB)\n\nSo \"implementation-defined\" ≠ \"undefined\" ≠ \"random\". It's: \"not fixed by the standard; each implementation chooses and documents; consistent within that implementation.\"\n\nSimple analogy: like a config option with a \"vendor default\" — the standard says \"the vendor must pick and write it down\", not \"whoever does whatever\".\n\nSo for underflow: MSVC's CRT and glibc each pick a behavior and document it; the standard only bounds it (|v| ≤ DBL_MIN). Not \"different depending on library version\" exactly — different per implementation (vendor), and a vendor could change it between versions (documented).\n\nAlso mention: `strtod` is the *library* (CRT), not the compiler. So the relevant \"implementation\" is MSVC's UCRT on Windows and glibc on Ubuntu (plus libc++/libstdc++ for from_chars). Actually for `from_chars` floating, the relevant implementation is the C++ standard library (MSVC STL / libstdc++) — but importantly, `from_chars` doesn't have this underflow asymmetry in the same way (its spec uses result_out_of_range). Hmm, actually from_chars's spec says \"if the parsed value is not in the range representable by the type of value, ... result_out_of_range\". Underflow → is the parsed value \"not representable\"? The mathematical value 1e-400 isn't representable in double, but the nearest is 0. This is the ambiguity. There's a known LWG discussion. I'll keep it as open.\n\nKeep it simple: three categories, implementation-defined = vendor picks + documents.\n\n**Point 2: inf/nan row removal + GCC confirmed.**\n\nThe user says: \"про gcc - у меня на виртуалке тесты проходили. так что оно проходит, это подтверждено. иначе бы упало по несоотв. ожидаемому результату\".\n\nLet me evaluate: Is that reasoning sound? I checked cases.cmake: \n- t3_c_inf_coord (line 79), t3_cpp_inf_coord (102), t4_c_inf_coord (149), t4_cpp_inf_coord (187) — not guarded by if(UNIX), so they run on Linux.\n- t3_extra_nan (77), t3_cpp_extra_nan (100), t4_extra_nan (146), t4_cpp_extra_nan (184) — also unguarded.\n- These use CONTAINS on ERR with the specific expected message.\n\nSo IF the Linux run executed these and they passed, then GCC (libstdc++) from_chars accepts inf/nan. The user says tests passed. So the empirical claim is well-founded — but I should verify one thing: did the Linux run actually execute the full suite including these? The journal says the clean clone on Ubuntu passed, and later vcpkg/gtest runs. I'll accept the user's statement, with a caveat: as long as the run included these cases (they're in cases.cmake unguarded → they'd be included unless the build failed earlier). Good. So I agree, and note the reasoning is exactly right: the assertion is on the specific stderr wording, so a difference (invalid_argument → parse_not_number) would have failed the test. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nWait — one caveat: does `--contains` comparison catch it? The expected message for extra_nan is \"слишком много координат...\", and if GCC returned invalid_argument the program would print \"числовые данные\"/\"Нечисловые данные\" — different string → CONTAINS fails. So yes. Good.\n\nHmm, but one subtlety: the message files are CP1251 and the stderr from a Linux program — the source literals are CP1251 bytes, so the program writes CP1251 bytes to stderr regardless of platform. So the byte comparison works on Linux. Good. Unless the program/check normalizes encoding. Fine.\n\nSo: agree, confirmed empirically. And note the row should be removed to keep the table as \"различия\". But then — do they lose the info? They might keep a one-liner in the narrative. Actually they said \"ок уберу строку и тогда таблица это различия\". Good. But I should note: then the entry loses the record that their tests depend on inf/nan parsing. Maybe suggest moving that fact into the narrative below the table (one line), not the table. Optional. Actually the info is valuable (it's why geometry.cpp:47 exists). Suggest a one-line note, not a row.\n\nHmm — wait. Actually, should the inf/nan row really be removed? The user wants the table = \"различия\". Since it's not a difference, yes remove. But it IS a worthwhile fact. I'll suggest: move to a sentence under the table (e.g., \"оба распознают inf/nan — ср. строка 'что распознаёт'; на MSVC и GCC подтверждено тестами\"). Good.\n\nAlso: line 2019 in the current journal says \"помимо всего надо проверить: p/P в hex формате в from_chars;\" — that's their existing TODO. And 2027-2031 now lists the open questions (they added them). Wait, line 2027-2031 says \"предлагает зафиксировать незакрытые вопросы, которые решатся собственным тестом parse_diff\" including \"inf/nan на GCC\". Since GCC is now confirmed, that open item should be removed from the list. Good catch for point 3.\n\n**Point 3: re-check the journal again.**\n\nCurrent version (lines 1992-2031). Changes since last read:\n- 2002: appended \"(пока это не проверено на практике)\" ✓ good.\n- 2007: fixed: \"при переполнении и errno становится ERANGE; при underflow - значение с mod(v) ≤ наименьшего нормализованного; установка ERANGE тоже implementation-defined\" ✓ correct now. Typo fixed.\n- 2010: inf/nan row removed ✓ (table now ends at 2009).\n- 2019: \"- помимо всего надо проверить: p/P в hex формате в from_chars;\" — this is now a standalone bullet after the explanatory paragraphs. But it's oddly placed: it's after \"что забыла дописать\" paragraph, as a separate bullet without a heading. Actually it's under \"*Что сделано*\"? Hmm, the structure: 1993 \"*Что сделано*\", then bullet 1994, then table, then paragraphs, then line 2019 \"- помимо всего надо проверить: p/P в hex формате в from_chars;\". Then 2021 \"**Что заметила при работе с deepseek**\". So 2019 is the last item of \"Что сделано\". It reads as a TODO. It duplicates 2028-2029 (open items). Could be consolidated. Minor.\n\n- 2027-2031: \"предлагает зафиксировать незакрытые вопросы, которые решатся собственным тестом parse_diff\" with 4 sub-bullets. Issues:\n  - These are written in third person \"предлагает\" (deepseek предлагает) — but they're now in \"Что заметила при работе с deepseek\". Actually it's a mix: 2021 is the heading \"Что заметила при работе с deepseek\", and 2027 \"предлагает зафиксировать...\" is a bullet under it. So it's describing that deepseek proposed to record open questions. That's OK as a \"what I noticed\" item, but the 4 sub-items are actually the project's open TASKS, not observations about deepseek. Placement is a bit off: they belong in \"Что сделано / что не получилось / TODO\", not in \"что заметила при работе с deepseek\". Suggest moving the 4 items to \"Что не получилось / открытые вопросы\" and keeping under \"что заметила\" only the meta-observation (\"deepseek предложил зафиксировать незакрытые вопросы — согласна\").\n  - Item \"inf/nan на GCC\" (2030) — now confirmed, remove.\n  - Item 2028 \"0x-префикс при fmt = general (утверждение в строке «hex-число»)\" ✓ still open.\n  - Item 2029 \"p/P-экспонента при fmt = hex опциональна\" ✓ still open, and duplicates 2019. Consolidate.\n\n- Line 2025: \"он сделал предположение про strtod ... гипотеза про from_chars - он вернёт result_out_of_range. пока ничто из этого не проверено\" ✓ good — now correctly marked as unverified (I earlier flagged 2023; they fixed it by splitting into 2025 with \"пока ничто из этого не проверено\"). \n\n- Line 1994: still \"(cppreference)\" — the underflow part came from the standard. Minor: rename to \"(cppreference + стандарт C)\". I raised this; they didn't change. Re-raise gently? Per the block: \"Если я отказываюсь от рекомендации без явной причины — вернись к ней на финальной проверке.\" They didn't explicitly refuse; maybe just didn't get to it. I'll re-raise once, briefly.\n\n- Line 2013-2016: \"исправляю всё это в таблице\" — still present; the table is already fixed. Minor stylistic. I raised it; re-raise? It's minor; mention briefly.\n\n- Missing: \"Что не получилось\" heading. Now the open questions are buried under \"что заметила\". Suggest a proper \"Что не получилось / открытые вопросы\" block.\n\n- Line 2005 \"параметры ф-ции\": now omits `base` (integer deferred). Fine. But the row header says \"параметры ф-ции\" and lists fmt — good for float. OK.\n\n- Line 1999 \"что распознаёт\" from_chars: \"два семейства перегрузок: integer и float-числа\". Since the table is now strictly \"различия\" of float, and they deferred integer, maybe the from_chars cell should say \"float-числа (а также целочисленные — отдельная перегрузка, сравнение с strtol отложено)\". Minor.\n\n- Check 2006 from_chars cell: \"ptr (указатель на last - первое за диапазоном, если всё распозналось. или на первый недопустимый символ)\". ✓ correct.\n\n- 2007 from_chars: \"ec == result_out_of_range и ptr указывает на первый символ, не вошедший в паттерн. value остаётся нетронутым\" ✓.\n\n- 2008 ✓.\n- 2009 ✓.\n\n- One more: the table title row \"| пункт | strtod | from_chars |\" — fine.\n\n- Also possible: line 2003 \"hex-число | strtod: воспринимает 0x, 0X | from_chars: также воспринимает, но если bitmask hex...\" — the \"также воспринимает\" for general is the open item (2028). OK.\n\nSo my checklist for point 3:\n(a) remove \"inf/nan на GCC\" from open items (confirmed by their Linux run) — and add one line that it's confirmed.\n(b) consolidate 2019 with 2029 (p/P) — duplicate.\n(c) move the 4 open items out of \"что заметила при работе с deepseek\" into a \"Что не получилось / открытые вопросы\" block; under \"что заметила\" leave the meta-observation.\n(d) re-raise: header \"(cppreference)\" → also standard; \"исправляю всё это в таблице\" tense.\n(e) minor: 1999 integer caveat.\n\nAlso, I should address whether removing the inf/nan row loses info — suggest a sentence.\n\nNow, for point 2, I confirmed via cases.cmake that the inf/nan tests are unguarded (run on Linux). Good — I can state that as verification.\n\nLet me also double check: is there a possibility that on Linux the `check` `--contains` normalizes and the message would still match even if from_chars differed? No — different message text. So confirmed.\n\nAlso: is `t2_nan_vertexes` relevant? That's C-side strtol (\"abc\" name). Not relevant.\n\nNow, the answer should be concise but cover 3 points. Let me write.\n\nFor point 1, explain simply with the three categories and an analogy. Keep it tight.\n\nLet me also make sure to note the distinction: implementation-defined is documented; and it can vary between *implementations* (UCRT vs glibc), and a vendor may change it in a newer version (documented in release notes). So \"разное в зависимости от версии библиотеки\" is partially right: not the *point* (the point is \"standard doesn't fix it; vendor does\"), but yes, it can change between versions since it's vendor's choice.\n\nGood.\n\nAlso for point 1, tie back: for underflow, the standard gives the bounding constraint only; the concrete value is UCRT's / glibc's documented choice.\n\nLet me write the answer. Plan mode: read-only, so I'll present as a plan/observations and offer to apply later.\n\nFormat: 3 numbered sections matching their questions. Then final review confidence.\n\nLet me be careful with the \"два семейства\" etc.\n\nAlso — I should mention I verified cases.cmake to back point 2 (shows the tests are in the suite for Linux). That's good evidence.\n\nWrite concisely.",
