hey ran it). Good.\n\nNow, let me formulate the answer: this is a review of the results. The user asked me to \"look at\" the file. So I should analyze and report findings, focusing on what closes the open questions and what corrects the journal.\n\nKey deliverables:\nA. Answers to open questions 1–3.\nB. Journal corrections needed (critical!):\n   - Row \"hex-число\" (line 2002): the claim \"также воспринимает [0x]\" is FALSE on MSVC — from_chars general parses `0` and stops at `x`, exactly like hex format. So the row's contrast collapses: on MSVC, general behaves like hex for the 0x prefix. → Needs correction; and note the standard's letter suggests otherwise, i.e., potential non-conformance / ambiguity. Cross-check GCC.\n   - Rows about `value остаётся нетронутым` (lines 2006, 2007): on MSVC, for `result_out_of_range`, value IS modified (inf on overflow, 0 on underflow). Only `invalid_argument` leaves it untouched. So \"value остаётся нетронутым\" holds only for invalid_argument. → Critical correction.\n   - Line 2001 (p/P hex optional) — confirmed, can remove \"(пока не проверено)\".\n   - Line 2018-2028 (underflow): strtod on MSVC returns 0.0 + ERANGE for gross underflow; subnormal (no ERANGE) near boundary. from_chars: result_out_of_range for gross, ok+subnormal near boundary. → concrete, can replace the hypothesis.\nC. Note the `hex` format's token-set surprise (`abc`→2748, `1e400`→123904) — not a strtod-difference per se but a demo.\nD. MSVC `%a` formatting quirk (no 0x) — so the output isn't directly comparable to GCC's; keep in mind.\nE. Remaining: run on GCC (Ubuntu) to see if from_chars behaves the same (esp. 0x-at-general and value-on-out_of_range). That's the cross-platform question.\n\nAlso: \"what does this mean for their programs?\" \n- Their `parse_point` uses `from_chars(first, ptr_end, dots[j])` and checks `ec != errc{}`. For \"1e400\", on MSVC ec=result_out_of_range → their code returns parse_not_number (message \"Нечисловые данные\")? Wait, what's their mapping? In geometry.cpp: line 42: `if (ec != std::errc{} || ...) return parse_not_number;`. So result_out_of_range → parse_not_number. Hmm, and then the isfinite check wouldn't catch it. But for a value like \"1e400\", the message would be \"Нечисловые данные\" rather than \"не конечное число\". Is that observable in tests? They have \"inf\" literal test (parse_not_finite). But not \"1e400\". So their behavior: \"1e400\" → parse_not_number. Fine but worth noting? Their task spec says non-finite should be a distinct message. Since 1e400 yields out_of_range, they'd report \"нечисловые данные\". Minor semantic. Could mention.\n\nHmm, wait: does their code hit isfinite? If ec != ok → return parse_not_number before isfinite. So overflow via scientific notation (1e400) → \"Нечисловые данные\" not \"не конечное\". But literal \"inf\" → ec ok, isfinite false → \"не конечное\". Slight inconsistency in user-facing messages. Worth flagging as a small note (it's the kind of thing the mentor might ask). But is it a \"critical\" journal issue? No, it's a code behavior note.\n\nAlso `0x10` for their parse_point with default general → from_chars parses 0, ptr at 'x', then `*ptr` = 'x' not space → parse_not_number. So \"0x10\" as coordinate → error. Fine.\n\nAlso: their code strips one leading '+' and then calls from_chars. For \"++3\" → after strip, from_chars sees \"+3\" → invalid_argument → error. Good.\n\nAlso interesting: their code skips leading whitespace manually, so from_chars' no-whitespace is handled. Good.\n\nNow, the \"value modified on out_of_range\" finding matters for their code? In their code, they check `ec != errc{}` first, so they don't rely on value. So no bug. But if someone relied on \"value unmodified\", they'd be surprised. Worth noting as the reason the spec statement matters.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. If MSVC sets value=inf on overflow, and their code returns on ec != ok, fine. If MSVC sets value=0 on underflow, also fine. So no impact. Good.\n\nLet me also double check the claim \"MSVC sets value=inf on overflow\" vs \"the spec says unmodified\". Let me re-read line 45: `ec=result_out_of_range value=inf [inf] class=inf value_changed=yes`. Yes.\n\nHmm, but is it possible that `{:a}` with inf prints \"[inf]\" and the `.17g` printed \"inf\", and the sentinel was actually unmodified but... no, if unmodified it would print -1.234...e+200. So modified.\n\nOK. So this is a genuine and important finding. Let me be careful in wording: [ФАКТ observed on MSVC, ВЫСОКИЙ]; conformance question [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ]. And I should suggest checking the exact standard wording (and possibly it's a known MSVC deviation). I could fetch cppreference again to quote the \"value is unmodified\" line — I already have it from the earlier fetch: \"If the pattern was matched, but the parsed value is not in the range representable by the type of value, returns value of type std::from_chars_result such that ec equals std::errc::result_out_of_range and ptr points at the first character not matching the pattern. value is unmodified.\" Yes, I have that quote. Good.\n\nSo: cppreference (and, per it, the standard) says value unmodified; MSVC modifies. Either MSVC is non-conforming or the standard changed. This is worth verification. Actually — hmm, I should be careful: maybe I misremember and the standard was changed by a defect report to allow modifying value. Let me flag it as \"requires verification against the standard text\"; recommend checking N4950 [charconv.from.chars]. I can offer to fetch the standard wording.\n\nHmm, actually, we could verify quickly by fetching the C++ standard draft (e.g., eel.is/c++draft/charconv.from.chars). Let me consider doing that — it's read-only and directly resolves whether MSVC is non-conforming. That would be valuable. Let me fetch https://eel.is/c++draft/charconv.from.chars.\n\nYes, let me do that to be rigorous. The user asked me to look at the result; providing the authoritative standard text on \"value 