          "text": "Now I have the authoritative C++ standard text (eel.is/c++draft, which mirrors the working draft). Key findings:\n\n1. **`value is unmodified`** is explicit in ¶1: \"If the parsed value is not in the range representable by the type of value, value is unmodified and the member ec of the return value is equal to errc::result_out_of_range.\"\n\nSo MSVC's behavior (value=inf on overflow, value=0 on underflow) **contradicts the standard** — it's a conformance deviation (bug) in MSVC's floating from_chars. Wow, that's a significant finding. Actually, hold on — let me double check that MSVC really does this, or whether it's an artifact of the probe. The probe: `double v = kSentinel; auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(...); print v`. For 1e400: value=inf. Definitely modified.\n\nHmm — is this a known MSVC bug? Possibly. Actually, wait. Let me reconsider: MSVC's from_chars for floating point... I recall an issue where MSVC sets value even on out_of_range. Hmm. There's indeed a known MSVC STL issue: \"from_chars for floating point sets value on out_of_range\" — could be. Anyway, empirically observed + standard says unmodified ⇒ deviation. I'll report as [ФАКТ observed MSVC + ФАКТ standard] and conclude MSVC non-conforming here. Confidence HIGH for \"MSVC modifies value\"; MEDIUM about \"it's a bug acknowledged by Microsoft\" (I haven't checked their issue tracker).\n\nHmm, actually, I should be a bit careful: is it possible the student compiled with an older/newer MSVC where behavior differs? Whatever — their output stands.\n\n2. **`0x` under general: the standard says... let me read carefully.**\n\n¶6: \"The pattern is the expected form of the subject sequence in the \"C\" locale, as described for strtod, except that:\n(6.1) the sign '+' may only appear in the exponent part;\n(6.2) if fmt has scientific set but not fixed, the otherwise optional exponent part shall appear;\n(6.3) if fmt has fixed set but not scientific, the optional exponent part shall not appear; and\n(6.4) **if fmt is chars_format::hex, the prefix \"0x\" or \"0X\" is assumed.** [Example 1: The string 0x123 is parsed to have the value 0 with remaining characters x123. — end example]\"\n\nInteresting! The standard's bullet 6.4 says: if fmt is hex, the prefix \"0x\" or \"0X\" is **assumed**. That's different wording from cppreference (\"the prefix is not permitted\"). \"is assumed\" means: for hex format, the input is treated as if the 0x were already stripped — i.e., you provide \"1.8p1\", not \"0x1.8p1\". And the example: \"0x123\" → value 0, remaining \"x123\". Consistent with observation.\n\nBut for `general`, there's NO exception about 0x. So per the standard, with general, the pattern \"as described for strtod\" includes the hex form with 0x prefix. So `from_chars(\"0x10\", general)` should parse 0x10 = 16! But MSVC parses 0 and leaves \"x10\". So MSVC is ALSO non-conforming here (or the standard's intent is ambiguous).\n\nHmm! So both of these are deviations? Let me double-check: with fmt=general (= scientific|fixed), does bullet 6.4 apply? No, fmt is not hex. So the hex-float form should be accepted like strtod → \"0x10\" → 16. MSVC gives 0. So MSVC deviates.\n\nHmm, but wait — this is a well-known thing. Let me think: is it actually a known ambiguity/LWG issue? There's LWG issue 3780 \"std::from_chars for floating-point types is not required to be able to parse hex floats\"? Hmm. Actually, I recall there IS an open issue about whether from_chars should accept hexadecimal floating-point under general. Let me think... \n\nActually the referenced \"See also: ISO/IEC 9899:2024, 7.24.2.6, 7.24.2.8\" (C23). Hmm.\n\nGiven the elephant: MSVC's output shows general does NOT accept 0x. And GCC? Unknown. This is now doubly interesting: if GCC DOES accept 0x under general, then the two implementations differ on \"0x10\" — a real cross-platform behavioral difference.\n\nHmm, but actually — I'm now not 100% sure MSVC is wrong. Let me reconsider: perhaps the intended reading is that the \"expected form ... as described for strtod\" refers only to the *decimal* floating constant production, and hex is only via fmt=hex. The example in 6.4 suggests hex is a *mode*, not an auto-detected form. But then a general-format input \"0x10\" would parse as \"0\" (decimal zero, then 'x' stops). Which is exactly MSVC's behavior! Hmm. So maybe MSVC's reading is that hex form requires fmt=hex, and under general the '0' parses and 'x' stops. \n\nBut strictly, bullet 6.4 only *modifies* the hex case (\"prefix is assumed\"), implying that with hex fmt you write without prefix. It doesn't explicitly *remove* hex support from general. The wording \"as described for strtod\" (which includes 0x floats) suggests general should support it. So there's genuine ambiguity → likely an LWG issue. I should present this as: the letter leans one way (general accepts 0x), MSVC implements the other (general does not), GCC unknown → so this is a real portability hazard, and it's worth checking GCC. Don't overclaim who's right; present both readings and the observation.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider once more. I found online memory: There's a StackOverflow / LWG discussion: \"std::from_chars cannot parse hexadecimal floating point literals unless chars_format::hex\" — and MSVC/GCC both behave as MSVC here. If both implementations behave that way, then it's likely the intended interpretation (or a common misreading). Anyway, the user will run GCC.\n\nActually, importantly: the student's earlier table statement (line 2002) \"также воспринимает [0x]\" is now shown FALSE on MSVC. That's the correction.\n\n3. **Integer preconditions**: base 2..36 ✓. \"no 0x prefix if base 16\" ✓, \"'-' is the only sign ... only if value has a signed type\" ✓, \"no 0b prefix if base 2\" (C++23 addition) — interesting, new. Good for the later strtol comparison.\n\n4. **Notes**: The standard says for floats: \"resulting value is one of at most two floating-point values closest...\" — matches.\n\n5. Also ¶1 explicitly: \"If the pattern allows for an optional sign, but the string has no digit characters following the sign, no characters match the pattern.\" → matches their \"5e+\"/\"+\" behavior. Actually \"5e+\"? Hmm, exponent needs digits; \"5e\" → per strtod, exponent optional; \"5e\" parses 5, remainder \"e\". Observed ✓.\n\nNow let me also double check: does the standard say anything about inf/nan for from_chars? It says \"as described for strtod\" → strtod accepts INF/NAN → so from_chars should too. Observed ✓ on MSVC. And it's implied by the standard, not an accident. Good.\n\nNow, the remaining open item for GCC: \n- whether GCC also modifies value on result_out_of_range (if GCC leaves it unmodified → cross-platform difference in *value* on error, though code shouldn't rely on it);\n- whether GCC accepts 0x under general (if GCC accepts, then cross-platform behavioral difference on inputs like \"0x10\"!). This is potentially a real bug-haunt for their code: if on GCC `from_chars(\"0x1p1\", general)` returns 2.0 with ec=ok and ptr=last, while on MSVC it returns 0 with ptr at 'x' (→ their code sees non-space 'x' → parse_not_number), then the same input \"0x1p1\" would be accepted on Linux and rejected on Windows. But their tests don't include hex input, so no test divergence — but it's a latent portability trap.\n\nSo recommendation: run the same probe on GCC and compare.\n\nAlso note: MSVC `%a` output lacks `0x` and formats subnormals oddly (`0.00000000007e8p-1022`), so when comparing with GCC, don't be confused.\n\nLet me now also answer the specific open questions:\n\nQ1 (0x at general): **MSVC: no** — parses 0, stops at 'x' (lines 66, 73). Standard's letter arguably says yes (strtod pattern includes hex) → ambiguity/likely LWG issue. → journal's \"также воспринимает\" is wrong for MSVC; mark as platform-dependent/unresolved; check GCC.\n\nQ2 (p/P at hex, optional): **confirmed MSVC: yes** (line 83 \"1.8\" hex→1.5; line 90 \"1.8p1\" hex→3.0). Remove \"(пока не проверено)\" — but note it's MSVC-confirmed, GCC to verify.\n\nQ3 (underflow): \n- strtod MSVC: gross underflow \"1e-400\" → 0.0, class=zero, **errno=ERANGE**; near-boundary \"1e-320\" → subnormal, **errno=0** (no ERANGE). \n- from_chars MSVC: gross → **ec=result_out_of_range, value=0**; near-boundary → **ec=ok, subnormal**.\nSo: the journal hypothesis \"from_chars вернёт result_out_of_range\" is TRUE for gross underflow, FALSE for near-boundary. And \"value unmodified\" is violated by MSVC.\n\nAlso for their program: \"1e-400\" on MSVC → from_chars ec=result_out_of_range → their code → parse_not_number (message \"Нечисловые данные\"), NOT the \"не конечное число\" branch. Whereas \"1e-320\" → ok, subnormal → passes isfinite → a valid point ≈0. The mentor might ask about this asymmetry. Worth noting.\n\nNow, journal corrections (plan):\n1. Row \"hex-число\" (2002): \"также воспринимает\" → \"по букве стандарта должен (паттерн как у strtod), но на MSVC при fmt=general 0x НЕ принимается: парсит 0, остаток 'x10'. Т.е. general у MSVC ведёт себя как hex в части префикса. Проверить GCC — если там принимает, это расхождение платформ.\" [ФАКТ на MSVC]\n2. Row 2001: remove \"(пока не проверено)\" → \"подтверждено на MSVC: '1.8' hex → 1.5, '1.8p1' hex → 3.0; экспонента опциональна\".\n3. Rows 2005/2006 (value unmodified): correct — on MSVC `value` не остаётся нетронутым при `result_out_of_range` (overflow → inf, underflow → 0), хотя стандарт [charconv.from.chars]/1 говорит \"value is unmodified\". При `invalid_argument` — нетронуто. → Это отклонение MSVC от стандарта (или как минимум от текста стандарта); проверить GCC.\n4. Underflow: replace hypothesis (2018-2020, 2028) with concrete: strtod MSVC gross→0.0+ERANGE, near→subnormal без ERANGE; from_chars MSVC gross→out_of_range+value=0, near→ok+subnormal. Отметить: \"следствие для программы: '1e-400' → ec=out_of_range → ветка нечисловых данных; '1e-320' → субнормаль, проходит isfinite\".\n5. Add: standard quotes (I now have the exact wording) — maybe add to journal.\n6. Note the `hex`-format demonstration: \"abc\" → 2748 (0xABC), \"1e400\" → 123904; that's not a difference row but a nice demonstration of what fmt=hex changes. Optional.\n7. Note MSVC `%a` quirk for when they run GCC.\n\nAlso: this is a strong \"Что заметила при работе с deepseek\" moment? Actually the probe was the student's own run. Hmm. Not about deepseek.\n\nAlso: I should mention that the probe found MSVC deviating from the standard, which is a genuinely valuable result — and worth recording in the journal as a finding (with the standard quote). And it justifies the \"не доверяй пересказу, иди в стандарт\" lesson.\n\nLet me also double-check one more: line 3 `from_chars general ec=ok value=42 ... ptr->''` for input '42' — good. Line 4 scientific → invalid_argument for '42' (exp required) ✓ matches bullet 6.2. Line 5 fixed → ok (no exponent needed) ✓. Line 6 hex → 66 (0x42) ✓.\n\nLine 26: fixed on \"5e2\" → value 5, ptr 'e2' ✓ (6.3).\nLine 34: hex on \"5e+2\" → 94 (0x5E), ptr '+2' ✓.\nLine 41: hex on \"5e\" → 94 (0x5E), ptr '' ✓.\nLine 47: fixed on \"1e400\" → 1, ptr 'e400' ✓.\nLine 48: hex on \"1e400\" → 123904 = 0x1E400 ✓ (0x1E400 = 123904? 0x1E400 = 1*16^4 + 14*16^3 + 4*16^2 = 65536 + 57344 + 1024 = 123904 ✓).\nLine 111: hex on \"abc\" → 2748 = 0xABC ✓ (10*256+11*16+12 = 2560+176+12=2748 ✓).\n\nAll consistent with the standard's bullet 6.3/6.4.\n\nNow, one more nuance worth noting: for `scientific` on \"42\", ec=invalid_argument (exp required). And for \" +5\" all formats invalid (leading space). ✓.\n\nAlso: `strtod` on \"1e-320\" gave errno=0 (no ERANGE) — so MSVC sets ERANGE only for gross underflow. Standard says it's implementation-defined → consistent.\n\nNow let me write the answer. Structure:\n\n1. Сначала — что это за билд (MSVC, судя по `%a` без 0x) — so they know.\n2. Ответы на 3 открытых вопроса.\n3. Находки, которые правят журнал (critical): hex-строка, value-unmodified, underflow конкретика.\n4. Что ещё видно (hex-формат меняет набор токенов; `%a` quirk).\n5. Что осталось: прогнать на GCC.\n6. План правок журнала (since plan mode, I can't edit).\n7. Финальная проверка / confidence.\n\nKeep it tight but complete. The user asked to \"look at it\" — they want analysis.\n\nI should be careful to flag [ФАКТ/ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ] and confidence.\n\nLet me also decide: the standard text I fetched is from eel.is/c++draft — that's the working draft (C++26-ish). The quote says \"as described for strtod\" and bullet 6.4 \"is assumed\". Good. I'll cite it.\n\nImportant: I should point out that the standard's ¶1 \"value is unmodified\" applies to both integer and floating overloads (it's ¶1, the common preamble). So MSVC's modifying value on result_out_of_range is a deviation. HOWEVER — caution: maybe MSVC's behavior is actually consistent if we interpret \"not in the range representable\" differently... no, 1e400 is definitely not representable. So deviation. But let me soften: \"похоже на отклонение MSVC; возможно, известный дефект — стоит свериться с их issue-трекером\". Mark [ФАКТ наблюдение, ВЫСОКИЙ]; [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ про статус бага, СРЕДНИЙ].\n\nAlso, subtle: for `1e-400`, MSVC sets value=0 and ec=result_out_of_range. Is 0 \"in range\"? Yes, 0 is representable. So per standard, should it even be out_of_range? The standard says \"If the parsed value is not in the range representable...\" — the parsed value 1e-400 is not representable, so out_of_range is right. And \"value is unmodified\". MSVC modified. So deviation confirmed for both overflow and underflow.\n\nBut note: near-boundary 1e-320 → not out_of_range (subnormal is representable). Fine.\n\nNow, is there any chance the standard changed such that underflow yields out_of_range and sets value? No — \"value is unmodified\".\n\nOK.\n\nLet me also mention impact on their own code: none for correctness (they gate on ec first), but if they ever rely on value-after-error, don't. And the user-visible asymmetry: literal \"inf\" → \"не конечное число\"; \"1e400\" → \"нечисловые данные\" (since ec != ok short-circuits before isfinite). Actually wait: in `parse_point`, for \"1e400\": from_chars returns result_out_of_range → line 42 `ec != errc{}` → return parse_not_number. So message \"Нечисловые данные\". Whereas \"inf\" → ec ok → isfinite false → \"не конечное число\". So two different diagnostics for two non-finite inputs. The task spec (§ 6.1) says \"не-конечное число\" case... Hmm, their spec: \"inf/nan — отдельный вывод\". \"1e400\" isn't literally tested. It's a minor consistency issue the mentor might notice. I'll flag it as a small note, low priority.\n\nLet me write.\n\nAlso note: the probe file parse_diff.cpp is in Desktop/diff, outside the repo — good, as planned. It compiled (exe+obj present). Note the .obj/.exe are artifacts; if they ever move it into the repo, .gitignore. Not needed.\n\nLet me write the answer now. I'll keep sections clear.\n\nOne more: the user's question was just \"посмотри\" — so report. Also they may want me to update the journal (as planned earlier). Since plan mode, I present the plan of edits and ask to proceed.\n\nLet me draft concisely.",
