ssion guard I mentioned.\n\nHmm wait — does C++ trim before parse_point? Let me check t3 main: it calls parse_point(temp) with the raw line? geometry.cpp parse_point skips leading whitespace in the while loop (line 23-24). So \" +3\" → skip space → ptr at '+' → strip '+' → from_chars(\"3\") → ok. So C++ accepts. Good. So a test \" +3\" (leading space + plus) would pass on both and guard the earlier bug. Actually the earlier bug (journal 2026-09-11) was in t1 (single number) where leading space wasn't trimmed. Let me check t1 main: they use trim + from_chars. The journal says they added trim from string_utils. So \" +5\" should now work for t1. A test would guard it. Currently no t1/t2 leading-space test.\n\nHmm, t2_empty_name = [\" \"] (a single space). That's a whitespace-only name → treated as empty. So whitespace-related for t2 name, but not leading whitespace before a number.\n\nOK.\n\nNow let me also consider: **`0x` recommendation**. The cleanest: reject hex on both.\n- C++ already rejects \"0x10\" (via non-space after parse). But wait — what about a token like \"0x\" followed by space, e.g. \"0x 2 3\"? from_chars(\"0x ...\") → parses 0, ptr at 'x' → reject. Fine.\n  - But there's a subtle case: \"0\" alone is fine; \"0x\" is rejected. Good.\n- C accepts \"0x10\" as 16. Need a guard. Options:\n  (i) Detect token starting with \"0x\"/\"0X\" and reject.\n  (ii) Don't use strtod's hex: pre-validate the token's characters (only [0-9], '+', '-', '.', 'e', 'E'). That's reimplementing the grammar — too much.\n  (iii) Accept that C and C++ differ — bad (parity).\n  So (i): in geometry.c parse_point, after skipping whitespace, if `pointer[0]=='0' && (pointer[1]=='x'||pointer[1]=='X')` → return PARSE_NOT_NUMBER. Also in the \"extra\" branch and in t4 radius (t4_filter_c/main.c uses strtod for radius). Need same guard there.\n  \n  Hmm, but where exactly? In parse_point loop, before strtod: check hex prefix. And in the trailing-extra branch. And in t4 radius parsing. Tha