'+' в экспоненте и 'E' — нет\n- 0x — нет (+ code divergence)\n- overflow 1e400 — нет\n- underflow 1e-400, 1e-320 — нет\n- 'infinity'/'nan(2)' — нет (optional)\n- запятая — нет (optional)\nPlus note: leading space + plus (\" +3\") as a regression guard for the t1 bug from 2026-09-11.\n\n### 3. (a) 0x\nExplain the parity divergence (C accepts 16.0, C++ rejects). Recommend rejecting on both; C++ already rejects; add C guard; add test.\nNote: don't use chars_format::hex (would make \"abc\" a number).\n\n### 4. (b) 1e400/inf\nExplain the two axes: (i) within C++ `inf` vs `1e400`; (ii) C vs C++.\nRecommend: add `result_out_of_range` branch in C++ → distinct message, same for overflow and underflow (can't split portably). C: detect via isfinite+errno / errno&&d==0.0. Tests.\nOr: simpler alternative (accept only literal inf/nan, don't chase underflow) — but then 1e-400 parity remains broken. Recommend the former.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the message design. If we unify `1e400` and `1e-400` into one \"out of range\" message, and `inf`/`nan` into \"non-finite\", then:\n- C++: ec==result_out_of_range → \"вне диапазона\"; else if !isfinite → \"не конечное\".\n- C: after strtod: if (errno==ERANGE && (d==0.0 || !isfinite(d))) → \"вне диапазона\"; else if (!isfinite(d)) → \"не конечное\".\nActually simpler C: \n```\nerrno = 0;\nd = strtod(...);\nif (errno == ERANGE && (d == 0.0 || !isfinite(d))) return PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE;\nif (!isfinite(d)) return PARSE_NOT_FINITE;\n```\nHmm, `errno==ERANGE && !isfinite` covers overflow; `errno==ERANGE && d==0` covers gross underflow. Note: for overflow, isfinite is false. So `errno==ERANGE` alone would catch both overflow and gross underflow, but ALSO `1e-320` on GCC (subnormal, ERANGE). So we need the extra condition to exclude subnormal: i.e., out_of_range = errno==ERANGE && (d==0.0 || !isfinite(d)). That excludes subnormals (d≠0, finite) → accepted. Good.\nThen non-finite = !isfinite(d) (and not out_of_range) → literal inf/nan. Good.\n\nThat matches C++. \n\nBut wait: on MSVC, `1e-320` → errno=0 (probe). So condition false → accepted. Good. On GCC, errno=ERANGE but d is subnormal → condition `d==0.0 || !isfinite` false → accepted. Good. Consistent.\n\nAnd `1e-400`: both platforms errno=ERANGE, d==0.0 → out_of_range. ✓ matches C++.\nAnd `1e400`: errno=ERANGE, d=inf → out_of_range. ✓ matches C++.\nAnd `inf`: errno=0, d=inf → not out_of_range → non-finite. ✓\nAnd `nan`: errno=0, d=nan → non-finite. ✓\n\nGreat, portable-for-these-cases. Add a caveat that this leans on errno which is implementation-defined, hence the explicit `d==0.0 || !isfinite` guard.\n\nHmm, one more: is `errno` set by strtod for overflow reliably on both? Probe: `1e400` MSVC errno=ERANGE, GCC errno=ERANGE. ✓. And the C standard says overflow sets ERANGE if MATH_ERRNO. Fine.\n\nAlso need `errno=0` reset before each call, and `#include <errno.h>`.\n\nThis is a solid, defensible plan. But it adds a new parse code (PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE = 5) on both sides, and new messages. That's a non-trivial change → the user should decide. I'll present it as recommended, with the simpler alternative.\n\nActually — hold on. Is it correct that `1e400` overflow gives `result_out_of_range` on both C++ compilers? Probe: yes (lines 45-46 both). ✓\n\nAnd `1e-400` → result_out_of_range both. ✓\n\nOK.\n\nNow, is adding a new error category worth it vs just mapping result_out_of_range to parse_not_number (current) and making C match by mapping its out-of-range to PARSE_NOT_NUMBER? That would give a single \"нечисловые данные\" for 1e400/1e-400/inf? No — inf/nan would still be non-finite. So the inconsistency `inf` (non-finite) vs `1e400` (not-a-number) remains. The user explicitly wants to fix that (\"хотя должно быть одно и то же\"). So we need a category that groups 1e400 with inf-ish? Or groups 1e400 and 1e-400 separately from inf?\n\nThe user says: \"если 1е400 / inf то там разные результаты хотя должно быть одно и то же\". So they think `1e400` and `