ntended\" value is 1e400, which is beyond double → it's effectively \"too large\". Reporting it as \"не конечное число\" is defensible (the represented value is inf).\n- `1e-400` underflows to 0; the intended value is tiny. Reporting \"не конечное\" is not defensible (0 is finite). But reporting \"вне диапазона\" is.\n\nSo (A) is semantically cleanest, at the cost of 1e400 ≠ inf category. (B) satisfies the user's stated wish but mislabels 1e-400.\n\nI think the right engineering answer: use `result_out_of_range` → \"вне представимого диапазона\" (A), and explain to the user that `1e400` and `inf` are NOT quite the same thing: `inf` is a finite-representation of infinity (a valid double value, just non-finite), while `1e400` is a **decimal literal that can't be represented** — from_chars legitimately reports different errors. So it's OK for them to differ; and mapping them together isn't portably possible without hacks.\n\nActually — is that true? Can they distinguish? For `1e400`, from_chars returns ec=result_out_of_range. For `inf`, ec=ok and value=inf. So the *inputs* are semantically different, and the program can legitimately produce different diagnostics. The user's intuition (\"should be same\") is worth challenging: it's actually acceptable (and standard-mandated) that they differ. Per anti-sycophancy, I should push back on the premise.\n\nSo my recommendation for (b):\n- Don't force `1e400` and `inf` to be the same; they're different classes (unrepresentable literal vs non-finite value). But DO fix the current labeling: `1e400` shouldn't be called \"нечисловые данные\" (it IS a number, just unrepresentable). Add a distinct \"вне представимого диапазона\" branch. That's the real defect: wrong diagnostic, not \"should be same as inf\".\n- And align C and C++.\n\nThat's a stronger, more honest answer. And it echoes the journal's own thought (2044-2046: differentiate inside the ec branch; but 2046 said \"и если переполнение - кидать также parse_not_finite\" — which I'm now challenging: merging overflow into non-finite would drag underflow along, since ec can't split them).\n\nYes! That's the key insight: the journal's proposed fix (2046: \"если переполнение - кидать parse_not_finite\") is **not implementable portably**, because `result_out_of_range` covers both overflow and underflow, and you can't tell them apart from the result. So I should flag that too.\n\nGreat, that's a substantive correction.\n\nNow, \"какие ещё места на доделать\":\n- parity 0x, underflow\n- new error category + messages on both sides\n- tests\n- the `%a`/value stuff: nothing to do in code (probe only)\n- Possibly: `strtod` accepting \"infinity\"/\"nan(2)\" vs from_chars — check parity (probe extension) if they want.\n- The `0x` guard could be in a helper.\n- Also: t4 radius has the same issues (get_radius, t4_filter_c/main.c:110).\n- Also: the C side `parse_point` uses `strtod` which is locale-dependent; if they ever call setlocale(LC_NUMERIC,...) it breaks — they don't. Fine.\n\nAlso, maybe: the journal's open thought about creating a helper for \"out of range\". Not needed.\n\nNow, one more test-worthy thing: **the exit code** for these cases (65) and the message. Covered by add_case.\n\nLet me also give concrete test input suggestions:\n- leading whitespace: `\" 5\"` (t1/t2), `\" 1.5 2.5 3.5\"` (t3), `\" 3 4 5\"` (t4).\n- exponent plus/uppercase: `\"1E+2 2 3\"` (t3), radius `\"1E+2\"`.\n- 0x: `\"0x10 2 3\"` (t3), `\"0x10\"` radius (t4).\n- overflow: `\"1e400 0 0\"`.\n- underflow: `\"1e-400 0 0\"`, `\"1e-320 0 0\"`.\n- optional: `\"1,5 2 3\"` (comma), `\"infinity 0 0\"`, `\"nan(2) 0 0\"`.\n\nAlso note: for shared expects between C and C++, the messages must match after the code change.\n\nNow let me be careful about a claim: does the C++ side currently reject \"0x10\"? Yes (parse_not_number). Does C accept? Yes (16.0). Let me double-check C's parse_point on \"0x10 2 3\":\n- i=0: strtod(\"0x10 2 3\") → 16, end at \" 2 3\". isfinite ok. end != pointer, *end == ' ' → ok. pointer=end, skip spaces → \"2 3\". i=1.\n- i=1: strtod → 2, etc. i=2. Then i=3. → accepted point (16,2,3). So C accepts.\nC++ parse_point(\"0x10 2 3\"):\n- skip spaces, ptr_start at '0'. first=ptr_start, not '+'. from_chars(\"0x10 2 3\", ptr_end, dots[0]) → parses \"0\", ptr at 'x'. ec ok. Then line 38: `ec==ok && ptr==ptr_end && j<2`? ptr != ptr_end, so no. Line 42: `ec != ok || (ptr != ptr_end && !isspace(*ptr))` → *ptr='x' not space → true → parse_not_number. Rejected.\nDivergence confirmed.\n\nGood.\n\nNow, are there other divergences? Let me think about \"5.\" and \".5\":\n- strtod(\"5.\") → 5.0, end after '.' (consumes \"5.\"). from_chars(\"5.\") → parses 5, ptr at '.'? Hmm — does from_chars consume the '.' if no digit follows? The pattern: digits optionally containing '.'. \"5.\" — the subject sequence per strtod: \"5.\" is valid (digits followed by '.' with no fractional digits) → strtod consumes \"5.\", value 5. from_chars: pattern identical to strtod → should also consume \"5.\"? Hmm. Not tested. Potential divergence but probably both same. Not worth chasing unless they want.\n- \".5\": strtod → 0.5; from_chars → 0.5? Probably. \nNot critical. Could add to probe if they care.\n\nAlso \"0\" vs \"-0\": both fine.\n\nNow, I think I have enough. Let me also quickly verify the C++ t3 message mapping to know where to add the branch. I have t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp: lines 61-80 (parse errors) and 117-123 (radius). For t3, main.cpp similar. I'll reference generically.\n\nLet me also note: adding a new parse code means updating geometry.hpp (parse_* constants), geometry.cpp, geometry.c, geometry.h, and the message mappings in 4 mains (t3_c, t3_cpp, t4_c, t4_cpp). That's a cross-cutting change — worth flagging as the \"architecture axis\": the error-model is a shared enum; adding a code touches all consumers. Per § 4.2, name the architectural axes before the fix. So I should say: this is not a local fix; it's an error-model change touching geometry + 4 mains + tests. And an alternative that avoids a new code: reuse `parse_not_number` for out-of-range (but then message \"нечисловые данные\" is the wrong diagnostic). Or reuse `parse_not_finite` (mislabels underflow). So a new code is the clean choice.\n\nHmm, or: keep two codes for out-of-range? No.\n\nOK, let me also mention: this touches the shared library ABI/enum — since it's just constants, fine.\n\nNow let me write the plan. It should be comprehensive but organized. I'll use sections and tables. Since the user asked in Russian, answer in Russian.\n\nAlso, plan mode: I must not edit; present the plan and ask for decisions. End with questions/tradeoffs and final confidence.\n\nLet me draft carefully but not excessively long. This is a big answer; I'll try to keep it scannable.\n\nLet me make sure to answer \"какие ещё места на доделать ты видишь\" thoroughly:\n1. Parity 0x (C accepts, C++ rejects) → guard + test.\n2. Parity underflow 1e-400 (C accepts, C++ rejects) → code + test.\n3. Overflow 1e400 mislabeled \"нечисловые данные\" (C++), while C says \"не конечное\" → unify + new code.\n4. Missing tests: leading whitespace, '+' in exponent, 'E', comma (optional), infinity/nan(2) (optional), underflow/overflow/hex.\n5. Journal: the proposed fix in 2044-2046 (\"переполнение → parse_not_finite\") is not portably implementable — flag.\n6. Optional: extend parse_diff with \"infinity\", \"nan(2)\", \"5.\", \".5\", \",\", huge exponent, to check parity before writing tests.\n7. Note: C's parse_point uses errno if we add out-of-range detection → must reset errno; also errno is impl-defined (document).\n\nAlso maybe: the C++ code manually strips '+' (geometry.cpp:32) and get_radius; the C side relies on strtod. Fine.\n\nAlso: one subtle bug-ish: C++ geometry.cpp line 38: `if (ec == std::errc{} && ptr == ptr_end && j < 2) return parse_too_few;` — for input \"1e400\" (single token, no more), from_chars returns out_of_range so not this. Fine.\n\nAlso: C++ geometry.cpp line 42 uses `std::isspace(*ptr)` — after a parsed number, if the next char is a space, continue. For \"0x10\", *ptr='x' → error. Good.\n\nAlright. Also note: the \"value modified on MSVC\" is irrelevant to tests (they don't read value) — confirm to the user this is not test-worthy, as they guessed.\n\nLet me write.\n\nI'll include a \"что тестируемо / что нет\" section referencing table rows.\n\nLet me produce the final answer.\n\nOne more: I should ask clarifying questions (plan mode responsibility): \n- Do they want a new error category (PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE) or reuse existing? \n- Do they want C/C++ parity enforced (affects C code + errno)?\n- Do they want to scope underflow/overflow at all (task spec doesn't require)?\nPresent as options.\n\nOK writing now. Keep reasonably tight.",
