        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now I can answer concretely.\n\nKey observation: `geometry.c` and `string_utils.c` are compiled into the **same** library targets:\n- `common_c_static` = STATIC geometry.c string_utils.c\n- `common_c_shared` = SHARED geometry.c string_utils.c\n- Similarly for C++.\n\nSo they are already in the same library. If geometry.c includes string_utils.h and calls get_string/is_empty/trim_string, the symbol is resolved **within the same archive/DLL** — no inter-library linking needed.\n\nSo the answer:\n- **Header dependency only**: geometry.c adds `#include \"string_utils.h\"`. That's it. No `target_link_libraries` change.\n- Because both .c are sources of the same target (`common_c_static` / `common_c_shared`), CMake compiles both and archives/links them together. The linker resolves `get_string` etc. from the same target's objects.\n- **When would you need to link one library to another?** If string_utils were a **separate target** (e.g., `add_library(string_utils ...)` and `add_library(geometry ...)`), then `geometry` would need `target_link_libraries(geometry PUBLIC string_utils)` (or PRIVATE if internal). For the static case, this adds string_utils' .lib to geometry's usage requirements; for the shared case, the DLL would link against string_utils' import lib, and consumers would transitively get it (PUBLIC).\n\nNow, a wrinkle: there IS a separate target in the CMake: `string_utils_c_static` / `string_utils_cpp_static` (lines 29-36) — used for t2. So string_utils.c is compiled into TWO targets: `common_c_static` (with geometry) and `string_utils_c_static` (alone). That's fine — a source can be in multiple targets; each target compiles it separately (duplicate object code, no ODR issue because they're separate libraries, though you must not link both into one exe with duplicate symbols... actually duplicate symbols across two static libs pulled into the same exe could cause \"multiply defined\" if both are linked and the linker pulls the same symbol. Hmm — for t1 they link common_c_static; for t2 they link string_utils_c_static. If some target linked BOTH, duplicate definitions of get_string/is_empty could arise. But that's a pre-existing design, not the question.)\n\nSo for geometry.c using string_utils: since geometry.c is in `common_c_static` alongside string_utils.c, no extra link. ✓\n\nBut also note: `common_c_static` and `string_utils_c_static` are separate targets compiling the same source. If a consumer linked both, duplicate symbols. Not relevant to the question but worth a caution if they ever consolidate.\n\nAlso the C++ side: they don't include string_utils from geometry.hpp? geometry.cpp could include string_utils.hpp — same library. Fine.\n\nThere's also a subtlety about **circular dependency**: if geometry and string_utils were separate targets AND string_utils used geometry, you'd get a cycle → static lib cycles are painful (need repeated libs or WGNU grouping), DLL cycles need import libs both ways. Currently they're fused, so no cycle. This is a good design point: because they're in one target, geometry↔string_utils cross-calls are fine.\n\nAlso: header include direction. Currently string_utils.h does not include geometry.h (journal: they decoupled). If geometry.c includes string_utils.h, the include graph is geometry.c → string_utils.h; string_utils.c → string_utils.h. No cycle. Fine.\n\nAlso: `COMMON_API` nuance — since both are in the same library, the macro for cross-library import/export isn't relevant *within* the library (when compiling the DLL, string_utils.c and geometry.c are compiled with COMMON_EXPORTS → both dllexport; geometry.c calling get_string within the same DLL resolves internally). But: does geometry.c calling a `__declspec(dllexport)` function from the same DLL work? Yes — within the same module, calls to an exported function resolve normally (the compiler sees it as dllexport, which implies it's defined in this module). Actually subtlety: when compiling the DLL, `COMMON_EXPORTS` is defined, so `COMMON_API` = `__declspec(dllexport)` for all declarations in string_utils.h. geometry.c includes string_utils.h with COMMON_EXPORTS → `get_string` declared dllexport. Calling it within the same translation unit set of the DLL is fine (dllexport implies the definition is in this module). ✓\n  But careful: with `/MD` and MSVC, calling a dllexport function defined in another .obj of the same DLL — the linker resolves it as a local symbol. Fine. No import lib needed.\n  Hmm, actually there's a known subtlety: `__declspec(dllexport)` on a *declaration* (not definition) in another TU of the same DLL — MSVC treats it as \"defined here\" and it works. Yes.\n\n  For the static lib case: COMMON_STATIC → COMMON_API empty. Fine.\n\n  For the consumer (main) of the DLL: they include geometry.h with dllimport (no COMMON_EXPORTS/COMMON_STATIC) → geometry symbols imported. If geometry.h exposed a function taking a `char*` from string_utils... not relevant. But if geometry.h itself included string_utils.h, then main would also see string_utils decls with dllimport — fine since they're exported too. Not the case.\n\nSo the answer is straightforward: **no, you don't link one library to another — because they're already in the same library target.**\n\nLet me also mention the general rule:\n- Include in code = compile-time dependency (header path).\n- Link = only when symbols live in a different binary (lib/dll target).\n- Same target → compiler+archiver/linker handle it internally.\n\nAnd when the answer would be \"yes\": if geometry were its own target separate from string_utils, then `target_link_libraries(...)`.\n\nAlso mention PUBLIC/PRIVATE/INTERFACE semantics briefly since their CMake uses them (journal). For internal (PRIVATE) use, PRIVATE is enough; if the dependency leaks through geometry's public headers (`geometry.h` includes `string_utils.h`), then PUBLIC.\n\nThat last point is important: **whether it's PRIVATE or PUBLIC depends on whether string_utils.h appears in geometry.h (public header) or only geometry.c (implementation)**. Since the plan is to use it inside geometry.c only (implementation), PRIVATE would suffice — but since they're the same target, it's moot.\n\nLet me also address the specific hypothetical: if they DID split them:\n- Static: `target_link_libraries(geometry_c_static PUBLIC string_utils_c_static)`. Link order handled by CMake.\n- Shared: geometry_c_shared would link string_utils' import lib, and consumers linking geometry_c_shared get string_utils transitively (PUBLIC).\n- Cycle (both use each other) across DLLs → need two import libs; MSVC supports it but it's ugly; better to fuse.\n\nNow, one more real concern in their current setup: string_utils.c is compiled into BOTH `common_c_static` and `string_utils_c_static`. If geometry.c calls get_string, then t1 links `common_c_static` (has both geometry+string_utils). t2 links `string_utils_c_static`. No target links both → no duplicate. But if a future target links both, you'd get duplicate symbols. Worth flagging as a caution.\n\nLet me check the root CMakeLists to see which targets link which. Actually not necessary for the answer; but I could mention the risk.\n\nLet me answer concisely, with the concrete evidence (common/CMakeLists.txt:6-7, 40-41).\n\nPlan mode: read-only; just answer.\n\nLet me structure:\n1. Короткий ответ: нет, при текущей нарезке — просто `#include`, линковку менять не надо, потому что оба .c уже источники одной цели.\n2. Почему (правило): include ≠ link; link нужен только когда символ в другой цели.\n3. Когда ответ был бы «да»: если разнести на отдельные таргеты → target_link_libraries, PUBLIC/PRIVATE (зависит от того, торчит ли string_utils.h в публичном заголовке geometry.h).\n4. Нюанс твоего проекта: string_utils.c уже в двух целях; не линковать обе в один exe (дубли символов). И про циклы.\n5. Про DLL/COMMON_API: внутри одной DLL вызов dllexport-функции резолвится локально, import-lib не нужен.\n\nKeep it tight