          "text": "The user asks two things:\n1. Briefly confirm: when a 4th coordinate is present but itself has an error (e.g., non-finite or such), we report the argument error, and only if it's correct do we say it's extra? — i.e., the principle: validate the 4th token; if it's not a valid number → not_number; if it's a valid number → too_much. And the question of non-finite: what about a 4th token that's \"inf\"/\"nan\"? Currently C++ treats parseable nan as too_much (not not_finite), per t3_extra_nan. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what the current/likely rule is:\n- 4th token not parseable as a number → parse_not_number.\n- 4th token parseable (even \"nan\"/\"inf\") → parse_too_much.\nSo non-finite 4th token → too_much (not not_finite), because we only care that there's a 4th token. That's the existing behavior (t3_extra_nan = \"1 2 3 nan\" → \"слишком много координат\").\n\nAnd for out_of_range 4th token (\"1 2 3 1e400\") → currently C++ gives not_number, C gives extra. The user's principle: \"we talk about the error in the argument only if it's an error; if it's correct, we say it's extra.\" So the question is whether out_of_range counts as \"error in the argument\" (→ not_number/out_of_range) or as \"correct enough to be extra\".\n\nHmm. Under the existing rule, \"nan\" (parseable but non-finite) → \"too_much\". So a 4th token is only \"not_number\" when it fails to parse a number at all (e.g., \"4k\"). A non-finite \"nan\" parses → too_much. So by that logic, \"1e400\" parses (as a number token) but overflows → is it \"parseable\"? from_chars returns ec=out_of_range, which is a parse failure (ec != ok). So under `ec != ok → not_number`, it's not_number. But \"nan\" gives ec=ok → too_much.\n\nSo there's an inconsistency in the rule: non-finite (ec ok) → too_much, but out_of_range (ec != ok) → not_number. Both are \"parsed a number token but the value is unusable\".\n\nThe user asks me to remind them of the principle. Let me articulate the current principle and