ues* of the double type (IEC 559 special values), yes. `from_chars` parses them with ec==ok. So yes. But is `inf` a \"number\"? The word \"number\" — inf is not a real number; nan isn't even comparable. In IEEE terms they're \"floating-point data\" not numbers. So calling inf a \"нормальное число\" is colloquially loose but operationally fine (it's a valid double value). The user said \"nan inf - Это нормальные числа\" — hmm, \"нормальные числа\" (normal numbers) — technically inf/nan are NOT normal numbers (in IEEE, \"normal\" means normalized finite; even \"normal\" has a specific meaning!). And they're not real numbers either. So the phrasing \"нормальные числа\" is imprecise: inf/nan are special *values* of the type, not numbers. But for the logic it doesn't matter — what matters is \"ec == ok\" (representable as a double value). I can note this terminology point: inf/nan are not \"numbers\" in the mathematical sense, but they ARE valid values of `double`; so if your criterion is \"parses into the type\", they pass.\n\nHole candidate 2: Is there a case where `from_chars` returns ec != ok for a value that IS representable? E.g., subnormal `1e-320` → ec==ok (representable). Fine. Any case? Underflow to zero `1e-400` → out_of_range, though `0.0` is representable. Hmm! Here's a subtle one: `1e-400` → from_chars returns result_out_of_range, but the *converted* value would be 0.0, which IS a representable double. So \"not representable\" is about the *mathematical value* (1e-400 ∉ doubles), not the rounded result. So the criterion is \"the mathematical value denoted by the numeral is representable\", not \"the conversion result is a valid double\". For `1e400`: value 1e400 not representable → out_of_range. For `1e-400`: value 1e-400 not representable → out_of_range. For `1e-320`: value is a subnormal, representable → ok. So the criterion is consistent: representability of the denoted real value. Good — this actually strengthens the user's \"не объект этого типа\" framing: the *value* denoted isn't an object of the type.\n\nSo I can validate the user's argument and sharpen the wording: \"валидное число\" = \"значение, которое этот литерал обозначает, представимо типом `double`\" (equivalently ec==ok). Under that, inf/nan pass (they're double values), 1e400/1e-400 fail (denoted value outside the type), letters fail for a different reason (not a numeral) — and the program correctly gives two distinct diagnostics for the two failure kinds.\n\nNow the residual \"reviewer might poke\" — is it fully gone? Let me reconsider whether there's still a legitimate objection:\n\n- A reviewer could ask: \"Why does `1 2 3 inf` say 'too many coordinates' instead of 'non-finite coordinate'?\" Answer: because pos 4 is judged at the \"is it a value of the type\" layer, not the coordinate-domain layer. Defensible, but must be documented. So the only residual is documentation/communication, not logic. So I should concede my point, and re-state it as \"the residual is only that the definition must be written down\".\n\n- Another possible reviewer question: \"Why is overflow 'вне диапазона' a different message from not-a-number, if both mean the token is unusable?\" Answer: because they're different causes; sysexits-ish differentiation; and the task wants specific diagnostics (§ 4.6). Fine.\n\nSo: the user's argument holds; I should say so plainly (not sycophantically, but because it's correct), sharpen two wording points (inf/nan aren't \"numbers\" mathematically; 1e400 and letters are NOT the same error category — code distinguishes them), and note one genuinely subtle case (underflow `1e-400`: denoted value not representable, but conversion result 0.0 is — so the criterion is representability of the *denoted value*, not of the rounded result; that's the precise formulation that makes your rule airtight).\n\nWait — is that last point actually a place where the rule could look inconsistent? Consider: at pos 4, `1e-400` → ec out_of_range → \"вне диапазона\". At pos 1-3, `1e-400` → out_of_range → \"вне диапазона\". Consistent. And `1e-320` (subnormal) → ec ok → at pos4 \"лишнее\"; at pos1-3 accepted. Consistent. Good.\n\nAlso, is there a case where the *rounded* value is non-representable but ec==ok? No.\n\nNow, one more potential hole in the user's argument: they say \"nan inf — Это нормальные числа, но негодные в координаты\". At pos 4 they're \"valid numbers\" → extra. But at pos 1-3 they're rejected via the finiteness filter (Слой 2). So the phrase \"нормальные числа\" conflicts with \"негодные в координаты\" only if you think \"normal number\" implies \"valid coordinate\". It doesn't. So fine — but the terminology \"нормальные числа\" should perhaps be \"значения типа double\" (since NaN/Inf aren't numbers in the mathematical sense, and \"normal\" has a strict IEEE meaning that excludes both zero/subnormal and inf/nan). Minor precision.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is it truly a good design that `inf` in pos 4 → \"too many coordinates\" while the same token in pos 3 → \"non-finite\"? One could argue for uniformity: apply Слой 2 everywhere, so `1 2 3 inf` → \"non-finite\". But then `1 2 3 4` still → \"too many\", and `1 2 3 inf` → \"non-finite\" — the user might prefer that. It's a design choice. But the user's current choice is coherent. I'll note it's a legitimate alternative but not an inconsistency.\n\nHmm, but earlier the test t3_extra_nan explicitly expects \"too many coordinates\" for \"1 2 3 nan\". So changing to Слой 2 everywhere would require changing that test. The user has already chosen. Fine.\n\nSo my answer: \n1. Concede: your argument is sound; at pos 4 the criterion is \"is the token a value of the type\" (ec==ok), and under that nan/inf are valid → extra; 1e400/1e-400 aren't → out_of_range. No internal inconsistency. My \"reviewer might poke\" reduces to \"document the criterion\", not a defect.\n2. Two wording refinements to make it airtight:\n   a. inf/nan are not \"numbers\" mathematically — they're special *values* of `double`. Better: \"литерал обозначает значение, представимое типом double\". Because \"нормальное число\" has a strict IEEE meaning (finite, nonzero, not subnormal) and inf/nan don't qualify.\n   b. 1e400 and letters are NOT the same category in your program: letters → нечисловые данные (invalid_argument); 1e400 → вне диапазона (out_of_range). Don't say \"как и буквы\" — the code correctly distinguishes them. Good.\n   c. Precise criterion: representability of the *denoted value*, not of the rounding result — otherwise `1e-400` (rounds to 0.0, a valid double) looks like it should pass. It doesn't because 1e-400 ∉ double. Worth stating, because it's the one case that could make someone question the rule.\n3. Residual: only that the two layers be documented. Optionally flag the alternative (apply finiteness at pos 4 too) as a valid-but-different choice requiring a test change.\n\nKeep it concise. Plan mode — analysis only.\n\nLet me write.\n\nAlso maybe: \"оспариваю ли я?\" — I should be honest: I don't find a substantive hole; I concede. That's the correct anti-sycophancy outcome (not agreeing for agreement's sake, but because the argument is valid). I'll say that explicitly.\n\nLet me also double-check the claim that `from_chars(\"inf\")` returns ec==ok — yes, from parse_diff (all formats ok for inf/nan on both compilers). And `from_chars(\"1e400\")` → result_out_of_range. Yes.\n\nAnd that `strtod(\"inf\")` sets errno? No (probe: errno=0). And `strtod(\"1e400\")` → errno=ERANGE. Yes. So C mirrors.\n\nGood.\n\nWrite concisely.",
