          "text": "The user asks a design question: \"по заключ. пробелам - как думаешь нормально будет добавить пробелы в конце к тем радиусам которые сейчас с пробелами в начале? чтобы доп. сущности не лепить\"\n\nSo: they want to add trailing spaces to the radii that currently have leading spaces, instead of creating additional entities/constants. I.e., reuse the existing `RAD_SPACE` (which is `\"   10\"`) and add trailing spaces → `\"   10   \"`, and reuse it for both leading and trailing testing. Avoid creating new `set(RAD_...)` constants.\n\nLet me think. Current case (from cases.cmake): `set(RAD_SPACE \"   10\")`, used in `t4_c_rad_space` and `t4_cpp_rad_space`, expecting `t4_test2`.\n\nProposal: change `RAD_SPACE` to have both leading and trailing spaces, e.g., `\"   10   \"`. Then the same case tests both ends at once. No new constant.\n\nIs that OK? Pros: fewer entities; one case covers both ends. Cons: it conflates leading and trailing — if the test fails, you don't know which end broke. But since both use the same trim mechanism, maybe acceptable. Also, existing tests already test leading only nowhere else... Actually the current `RAD_SPACE \"   10\"` tests leading. If changed to both, leading-only coverage remains implicitly (leading is still there). And trailing gains coverage.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: does one case covering both ends reduce diagnostic? For a course project, a single \"both ends\" case is fine, especially to avoid extra entities. But the guidance § \"тесты\" often prefers isolated cases. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: keep `RAD_SPACE \"   10\"` and add trailing to it → `\"   10   \"`. Then there's no pure-leading and no pure-trailing case. If trimming is implemented via the same first/last pointer logic, both are exercised simultaneously.\n\nTrade-off: If later someone breaks leading-only handling but not trailing, the combined test still catches it (the combined input has leading). If they break trailing-only, also caught. So combined catches both (as long as the implementation doesn't have independent bugs that cancel). So combined is acceptable coverage-wise.\n\nHowever, the \"2 3\" test (embedded space) is separate.\n\nAlso, another thought: they might want to reuse `RAD_SPACE` for the leading+trailing test, and maybe the `t4_rad_space` name stays. Fine.\n\nBut there's a concern: the expect for the test. Since the radius is `\"   10   \"` (with trailing spaces) and the program prints `argv[1]` in error messages — but this is a SUCCESS case (radius 10 accepted → output t4_test2). So no message printing. Expect = t4_test2. ✓ No trailing-space expect issue.\n\nBut if a test with trailing spaces is an ERROR case (e.g., `\"0x10   \"`), the message would include trailing spaces → expect tricky. They're not doing that.\n\nSo proposal: change `RAD_SPACE` to `\"   10   \"` and keep one case (C + C++). That covers leading+trailing in one test. Normal, no extra entities.\n\nIs it \"нормально\"? I'd say yes, acceptable — with one caveat: it doesn't isolate which end fails. If they want isolation without new constants, they could add trailing spaces to the *existing* leading one and rely on the fact that the mechanism is shared. Or use existing `RAD_POSITIVE`/`RAD_NEG` etc. and add spaces? Hmm.\n\nActually, a cleaner idea to avoid new entities AND isolate: modify the existing `RAD_SPACE` to include only trailing (drop leading) and rely on... no, then leading loses coverage. Hmm.\n\nOr: reuse `RAD_SPACE` for leading+trailing, and additionally note that leading-only is already covered by... is there another leading-space radius test? No. So combined is the way.\n\nActually — there's a subtle point: the coordinate tests already cover leading spaces (`t3/t4_leading_spaces`). But for the radius path specifically, only `RAD_SPACE` covers it. So combining leading+trailing into one radius case is fine.\n\nAlso: should the value be `\"   10   \"` or `\" 10 \"`? Minor. Any value with a valid number works; use the same number (10) so the expect stays `t4_test2` (radius 10).\n\nWait — need to double check: with radius 10, t4_test2 output is \"4.000 5.000 5.000\" (only point (4,5,5) with dist ~8.12 < 10). ✓ So expect t4_test2 unchanged. Good — no new expect either. \n\nSo the proposal avoids new constants AND new expects. \n\nMy answer: Yes, that's reasonable — change `RAD_SPACE` to have both leading and trailing spaces (e.g., `\"   10   \"`), keep the same value (10) → expect `t4_test2` unchanged, no new entities. Caveat: the case no longer isolates which end is broken; acceptable since both ends go through the same skip logic. If isolation matters later, add a separate case then.\n\nAlso mention: this is only a change to `set(RAD_SPACE ...)` in cases.cmake (one line) — no new constant, no new expect. Good.\n\nBut wait — plan mode: I must not edit. I just advise. The user will edit.\n\nAlso, is there any risk with `set(RAD_SPACE \"   10   \")` in CMake? It's a quoted string → preserved. In `add_test(${RAD_SPACE})` unquoted expansion of a variable containing spaces → CMake will split it into multiple arguments! Because unquoted `${args}` expansion splits on spaces (it's a list). Hmm!! This is critical.\n\nLet me check: in `add_case`, the `args` parameter receives `\"${RAD_SPACE}\"` (quoted at the call site). Inside `add_case`, `add_test(... ${args})` — unquoted `${args}`. If `args` is a string containing spaces, unquoted expansion splits it on `;` (list separator), not spaces. CMake list separator is `;`. A string \"   10   \" has no `;` → remains one element? Hmm.\n\nActually, CMake variable expansion: unquoted `${var}` expands to the value, and then it's split into list elements by `;`. Spaces within the value are NOT list separators. So `\"   10   \"` → one element \"   10   \". So it stays one argument. Good.\n\nBut wait — the earlier journal (2026-09-14) discussed `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` → unquoted → CMake stores \"2;3\"? Actually `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` with multiple args → the variable becomes the list \"2;3\". Then `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}` unquoted → splits into \"2\",\"3\" → two arguments. And `set(RAD \"2 3\")` → variable = \"2 3\" (single string, no ;) → unquoted expansion → one element \"2 3\". So yes, spaces alone don't split; semicolons do.\n\nSo `set(RAD_SPACE \"   10   \")` → variable \"   10   \" → one argument. ✓ The program receives argv[1] = \"   10   \". Good.\n\nBut careful: when passing through `run_case` on Windows (cmdline), the arg with spaces must be quoted — they fixed run_case to quote. So \"   10   \" would be quoted → preserved. On Linux execv preserves. ✓\n\nSo changing RAD_SPACE to both-ends is safe for the test harness.\n\nHmm — but also: does the value with leading/trailing spaces survive through CMake to CTest's generated file and run_case? The generated `CTestTestfile.cmake` will have the argument quoted appropriately by CMake (it quotes args with spaces). Then ctest passes it as one arg. Then run_case quotes it for the child. OK.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety: CMake's `add_test(NAME x COMMAND ... ${args})` — if args contains a value with spaces, CMake stores it as one command argument (CMake handles quoting in the generated file). Yes.\n\nSo my advice stands.\n\nLet me also consider: is it better to test leading and trailing in ONE case, or keep them separate? The user explicitly wants to avoid extra entities. So one combined case. I'll agree, with the caveat about isolation.\n\nAlso, note: the coordinate tests might also want trailing-space radius? No.\n\nSo answer:\n- Yes, normal. Change `set(RAD_SPACE \"   10   \")` (same number 10 → expect `t4_test2` unchanged, no new constant/expect).\n- One case now checks both ends; no isolation, but both go through the same skip logic, so acceptable. If a failure ever needs isolating, split then.\n- Verify no harness issue: spaces alone don't split CMake lists (separator is `;`), so it remains ONE argv; run_case quotes it (already fixed). On Linux execv preserves. So the value reaches the program intact.\n- One nuance: this combined case only covers \"both ends at once\"; a pure-leading or pure-trailing regression could theoretically be masked only if the two bugs cancelled — unlikely with a single skip mechanism. Fine.\n\nAlso mention: if they want isolation cheaply without new constants, they could put trailing spaces on one of the existing *success* radii... but that's the same thing.\n\nLet me keep it concise. Plan mode: just advise.\n\nLet me write.",
